      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>2 McDOUGALL'S Patellt SELF-RAISING FLOUR BEIV Y.l SYREY</head>
        <p>2 McDOUGALL'S Patellt SELF-RAISING FLOUR BEIV Y.l SYREY</p>
        <p>CON STIPATION FOR 16 YEARS. [ALL RIGHTS RESETIVED.I Chas. Forde's Bile Beans Cure a Desperate THE DISAPPEARANCE Case OF BETTY.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. J. Aldridge, wife of a shoe operative, living at 45, Midland-road, Olney, Bucks., said to a " Northampton Mercury" reporter : " I suffered from constipation and biliousness for quite sixteen years. I bad terrible pains in my head. My appetite left me, and when I did eat anything I was continually sick afterwards. The acuteness of the pains in my head sometimes made me quite distracted. I was always dread- fully cold and was continually shivering, vet I daren't go near a fire because it always brought on the sickness and pains in my head. When the worst attacks came on I had to go to bed for two or three days. I couldn't raise my head from the pillow, and even the thought of food made me sick. I was an out-patient at the hospital, but I received very little benefit. The constipation made me feel very depressed and miserable. "Chas. Ferde's Bile Beans were strongly recom- mended, so I tried them, and soon found that I was improving. I became less constipated, Lite bilious attacks grew less, the headaches less acute, and my appetite gradually returned. Con- tinuing with `Chas. Forde's' I rapidly regained health and strength until I had neither ache nor pain in my body. I can honestly say it was Chas. Forde's Bile Beans alone that cured me, and I recommend them to everyone I know." To show how complete is Mrs. Aldridge's cure she has just ciritten :—" It is three years since Chas. Forde's Bile Beans cured rue, and since then I have had no return of the constipation or biliousness. My health is excellent." Chas. Forde's Bile Beans are the lasting cure for constipation and such ailments as anternia, debility, indigestion, biliousness, liver troubles, piles, pimples, skin eruptions, blood impurities, palpitation, sleeplessness, headaches, neuralgia, and nervousness. Price is. ild. or 2s. Od. per box.</p>
        <p>' BY THE REV. S. N. SEDGWICK,</p>
        <p>author of "Petronilla," "The Human Ape," "A Daughter of the Druids," "Cystennyn the ; Murderer," "The Red Spider," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>"Why, father, you want to treat me as it I was a simpering miss of a hundred years ago— all crinolines and sham hysterics—ready to curtsey and blush, and say "Thank you, kindly, sir,' when the old man brought in any young beau he thought proper, saying, Here, there's a husband for you! "Don't you be impertinent, miss. I've set my heart on the match. He's the son of my 'ear old chum who worked side by side with me for years, till he emigrated. Now he's rich, and so am I, and all the way across the sea he sends his eon, saying, 'Can't we arrange a wedding ? ' Let me see, where', the letter. Ah ! here we are. 'l've given the tip to Barry, and he's a good, dutiful fellow and promises me to do what he can--' "</p>
        <p>"A great hulking goose of a Colonial, without mind to call his own! I simply decline the suggestion."</p>
        <p>betty jumped off the table, where she was swinging a determined little foot, and ran to her father. He, poor man, with a gouty foot upon a stool, was on the verge of an explosion. Betty threw her arms round his neck. "Now, dear old daddy," she began, "don't be so vexing. I promise you I'll be kind to him when he comes down here, but I will not have it taken for granted that he's only to speak to have me fall obediently into his arms. I won't do it!</p>
        <p>Unfortunately, she touched his knee, and a twinge of horrible pain shot up from that red- hot toe. Mr. Partingdon pushed his daughter away, and, I'm sorry to say, he swore. "Don't come round me," he shouted. "If you don't know what a daughter's duty is—` love, honour, and obey' " (getting his ideas a little mixed)—"l'll have nothing to do with you. I'll —l'll disinherit you; I'll cut you off with a mere pittance!" He paused to get his breath. "I know what it is. You're a giddy young fool— you're infatuated with that coxcomb of a Lord Paginet ! Upon my soul! I shouldn't be surprised to hear you've been carrying on with him already behind my back; though you know as well as I ' do what a reputation he bears in the county. Oh, you obstinate young minx!" The accusation was utterly untrue, but Betty seemed to have inherited her father's temper, and his words, especially the profane ones, goaded her into replying hotly:</p>
        <p>. "You can think what you like, father. If I like Lord Paginet, I'll marry him willy-nilly. And I tell you I will not be bullied or brow- beaten into seeing this rawboned Harry Ricardo —or whatever his horrid name is."</p>
        <p>Her lips quivered a little. A woman would have known just how to end the scene; but Betty's mother was dead, and her father only added fuel to her fire.</p>
        <p>She burst out of the room at last. "I tell you what it is, father," she cried. "If you invite that man down here go out of the house, as sure as my name's Betty ! " Whereupon, while she rushed upstairs and cried her eyes out upon her bed, the old man, with angry determination draped his desk towards him and wrote then and there to the White -Star office, to tell Harry to come straight from the docks to Silborne.</p>
        <p>Two days later the young Colonial, broad- shouldered and bronzed, received the letter as soon as he stepped ashore. He read it with pleasure, and wired immediately that he would be down by the evening train. And so it came about that Betty, accustomed to open all telegrams, was the first to read the words. "Thanks for invite. Reach 3ilborne 8.30 to-night. Ricardo."</p>
        <p>Those two days had been spent by Betty in a chilly and reserved silence. That was her way of bringing her father to his knees. 'Unfortu- nately, these tactics quite failed; for the old man, when he had calmed down, chuckled over his little scheme, and Betty was the more hurt at ending him, instead of capitulating, evidently enjoying her assumption of haughty coldness. Now she stood in the hall, the telegram in her hand. "So you did invite him, after all," she said, softly. "Very well." And in a silly, perverse mood, she rushed upstairs to her room. When the carriage drove back from the station, with the young man inside Mr. Partingdon dis- covered that his daughter clad kept her word—she had completely disappeared. Harry saw that the old man was evidently dis- turbed at dinner. He had sent his butler out quietly to look for the girl ; but it was not till hour after hour had gone by, and the man had returned with no news, that Harry heard the whole story. "It's all my fault," said Mr. Partingdon—"my confounded temper! But what are we to do? She's taken some things from her room, and she's left me this note."</p>
        <p>He read it aloud. "I told you I should go if you asked that man down here. So I've gone. I'll keep myself—and I'll marry whom I like.— BETTY."</p>
        <p>"Seems to have a bit of a temper herself, sir," said Harry, with a smile. "I shouldn't fret about it; she evidently knows how to take care of herself. If you'd let me, I'd go and make some inquiries. We can surely find where she's gone." He drove to the station, but learnt nothing of her. Silborne is five miles from the Blackton Junction, and Harry drove on there. To his joy, the stationmaster identified her as having gone to London by the afternoon express. "Perhaps she's at her aunt's' said Mr. Partington ; "they were always fond of one another."</p>
        <p>"Of course she is," answered Harry. "By Jove l she's a game little girl, air. Of course, I couldn't expect her to fall down and worship me the moment I arrived. It's a pity yob told her what my father said. I've always liked her— judging from her photograph, you know, the one you sent to the govei nor last year—and I told him that, so far, I was unattached, and would have a look at her. You understand, sir, it was a joke. I wouldn't have forced unwelcome atten- tions."</p>
        <p>"I know, my boy, I know. I hope it will come out all right. Yet I ought to have been wiser. Your father and I were bosom friends, and I thought if I could just give her a hint it might quicken things up. "A very pretty sort of a hint you must have given," thought Harry, as he went to bed; "but she's put me on my mettle, and I'll try her, anyhow. By Jove! I will." But a wire, reply paid, next morning brought the alarming news that the London aunt knew nothing of Betty's whereabouts. Mr. Partingdcn was for going to London by the next train, but his doctor absolutely forbade his leaving the house. So once again, not without a secret delight, Harry volunteered to go up to town and set inquiries on foot. He would advertise in the agony columns of all the papers "Come back and all shall be forgiven." He'd employ detectives— anything and everything to relieve the old man's anxiety. The evening came, bringing a new telegram: "No news. Shall stay in town." And the next day the advertisement appeared. It seems that Betty was on the look-out for it, and at the thought of her father's anxiety she relented so far as to send a letter, which reached Silborne at night: "Dear father, —I saw your advertisement. I'm quite safe, and have found work at once. But I won't come back—just yet—while that rough cowboy's in the house." Harry laughed as he heard it on his return. "She's safe, any way," he said; "that's some- thing. I suppose I'm the cowboy—gad! that's good. Well, anyhow, we've got a clue. I wonder where she is ? " The postmark was Covent Garden; it told nothing, and there was no address. "What could she do for a living, sir ? " Harry asked after a moment. "She could do some typewriting," said the old man, brokenly. "She has kept my oorre- spondence for a year. Or governessing, I suppose." "Anything else? Tell me everything she used to do. I don't think she'd touch the governess business too much submissiveness necessary-." "She played the piano. of course," said Mr. Partingdon, doubtfully. "Tennis, photography had a camera--" The young man sat up suddenly. "What make was it, sir ?" • "Why of course, it was one of yours, Harry— _</p>
        <p>Clie Brisbane Optical Company. We always sent there for films and things." "Our London branch is in King- street, Covent Garden," said Harry, impulsively. "As a matter of fact, we're not satisfied with our London manager. That's really why I'm in England. Did she know that it was our firm ? You know we trade under a different name."</p>
        <p>"Sure to have done, my boy." "Well, upon my soul !I'll try there to-morrov. I know we have plenty of typewriters in the office, and it's likely that she'd go to the business places she knew in town. If this was her hobby, it's ten to one it would come into her head to see if she couldn't turn it to account. If she's not there, I'll try the other firms you do business with—the Stores, and so on. You give me a list; but I have a sort of conviction that I've griesee, right, bang off the first time! It's almost a certainty, if she knew it was really our firm." "I don't see that," said the old man, though he was struck with Harry's assurance. The younger man hesitated and blushed. "Well, it's like my conceit to suppose so," he said. "But I've got it into my head that she'd keep an eye on the place, anyhow, just to see what I was like. She'd guess I should come there. If she got a berth, by any luck, she'd score off me pretty well, for I shouldn't be expected to scrutinise every clerk in the place. First thing to-morrow I'm off to King-street." "It's the merest chance," said Betty's father, "though I own she's just the sort to do it." "If it's true, sir, she's a jolly smart girl, and her picture tells me that already. I've leoked at it sometimes and tried to imagine what that girl's voice is like, how she moves her hands--" He broke off shamefacedly, not wanting the father to know how he had pondered over the photograph until he had fallen in love with the lady it represented. _ .</p>
        <p>"'lf it slmuld be true, sir," he continued, "and she's safe, I wonder whether you would mind my waiting a bit before telling her the game's up. I should like to score off her a bit. I think I could work it—and I'd take care no harm came to her."</p>
        <p>The old man giasped his hand. "My boy," he said, "it may be a wild-goose chase, after all, though you've almost persuaded me into thinking it's true. But if it should be correct, why, when once I know she's looked after and is safe, you can pay her back how you like. lnerve her right, the designing young jade! "</p>
        <p>"Does Miss Ricardo require any lunch?' he asked, tentatively. The manager stored; Betty blushed. "Miss Ricardo?" roared the manager. "What a name —and—what a ninoompoop ! This lady's a clerk, like yourself, Jones. But she won't require any lunch. She's going out with me, aren't you, dear?" His familiarity made the girl writhe inwardly; but it was all part of the game to her, so she said, "Yes, thank you, sir.'</p>
        <p>The errands executed, it rather surprised the manager to see young Jones walk into his pot restaurant, and sit down with his back to the pair at the other end of the room. He nudged Betty. "That's a silly fool, Miss Betty, isn't he? Corning here on £1 a week! Why, he'll 'ave to dine on dry bread after this!" The manager's assiduous attentions increased as the days went by. He insisted upon seeing Betty home. He forced her to take his arm; he went so far as to whisper rather over fulsome compliments in her pretty ears. Betty found him almost too oppressive; but she made up her mind she would not give up the game until young Ricardo had appeared at the oftice. Meanwhile, Jones, wearing a patient and timid look, had to do the lowest work of the ofnee— sweeping the floors, taking down the shutters, and licking the stamps. He was always at hand in the morning with a nervous bow to greet Betty; he always went round the house at night, saying good-bye to all the employees. This _ _</p>
        <p>No one knows what forces they are that draw mail and woman together out or all the woild: but that there is some such mysterious pow.,r goes without saving. 1+ 0- ,r Card. iih</p>
        <p>caused an endless amount of fnu. thougb Betty.</p>
        <p>—More truly, faitty ItlcAuno." "Do you Minn 1 am going to stay here alone?" Betty's eyes danced as she replied to this The man looked at her, then slipped to the letter. She looked forward to seeing the young door and locked it. His manner was brutal. "I Colonial from the advantage of her office stool, think so," he said. "'Ere are the girls; they'll Later that day Mr. Harry Jones turned up. A do for gooseberries." He pulled a photograph tall, dark young fellow' evidently very shy. from the wall and threw it to her; then She caught a glimpse of hire over the partition; deliberately poured out a glass and drank it. and, later on, she overheard a certain amount of "Ah!" he continued, "that's better. Now come, chaff from the other clerks, shewing that they my girl, all's fair in love an' war, they say. had found him a good butt for their wit. own I've played a little trick on you. You see, Mr. Amoss treated him with contempt. it's like this. I love you—l do that! I've never "From the country, Mr. Jones?" he inquired, seen a girl like you before. I know more about superciliously. ' you than you think. I ain't blind, and I've "Yes, sir. made a few inquiries. Well now, I'm leaving "Ah! Any experience ?" , the business to-night. Young Ricardo's come ; "No, sir; not in this line." ;to England to see why my returns 'aven't been "Turnip-boeing, I suppose? Ha-ha! Well, we quite so good. I've made a bit on my own, and are bound to take you in. Mr. Ricardo doesn't I've taken two berths to America. One for me, seem to understand the duties a a clerk. The and one for you. I'm going to marry you when junior's place is open for you. You must make we get there. You're a beauty! " ; yourself pretty useful all round." Betty sprang to the door and shook it. "Let ; Poor Mr. Jones—nervous country lad—found me go! " she cried. himself compelled to go out and get sundry ; Amon pulled her away. "Wait a bit," he ; dinners at one o'clock. Thinking he would be said, his eyes blazing. "I think you'll consent to doing the right thing, he came into the manager's come with me. If you don't, I'll go without room. you—but you'll never go back to your father _ _ .</p>
        <p>again." There was no mistaking his threat. Betty rushed to the old-fashioned French window. It was old and worn, and she hoped to push it ' open. "Help, help! " she screamed at the top of her voice, as Amuse sprang upon her and dragged her backwards, his breath hot upon her face, his arms tightly round her. She, almost fainting, terrified, blanched, relight as well as she could. He put a:hand over I her mouth.</p>
        <p>At that moment there was a shout from outside, the window was burst open, and a man, leaping in, threw himself upon the manager. It was young Jones. The manager closed with him. Tho table went over in the struggle. Jones caught his foot in it and tripped. Amoss fell with him, and seizing one of the knives which dropped to the floor with the dgris of the meal, cut furiously at his adversary with it. The blade pierced his shoulder—the blood spurted out—he loosed his grip. But as he slipped back to the ground, and Amos rose with a cry of triumph, a couple of policemen ran in and seiled him. A moment later he was handcuffed. "Not hurt badly, Mr. Ricardo ?" said one, stooping to the wounded man. "Sorry we were so late—the rest are outside." Ricardo! Betty looked down amazed. Who was this ? "I'm afraid I am," said Harry; "he's cut tin. artery. Put a tourniquet on as fast —as fast--" His voice faded away, .and he lose consciousness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR MOVEMENT.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>A Year's Work in Warrington.</p>
        <p>In place of the annual Convention in connection with the Warrington and District Union of Young People's Societies of Christian Endeavour, a united consecration meeting was held in Wycliffe Memorial Hall on Tuesday evening. There was a good attend- ance. At the outset the Rev. F. Vaughan (retiring president) presided and was supported by the Rev. J. L. Milne (the newly-elected president) and Mr. W. R. Beswick (hon. secretary). The CHAIRMAN, in referring to the work of the past year, said he could not but give thanks to God for the blessing and inspiration to his own Christian life and ministry which the office of president had enabled him to realize. The work had been blessed by God and the labours of the various soc•eties were testimony of practical Christian light and experience. He wished his successor in the chair a very happy and prosperous year of office. The regularity of Mr. Milne's attend- ances at committee meetings spoke eloquently of his practical interest in the work of the Union. The Rev. J. L. MILNE then took the chair. He said he felt the office to be one of great responsibility, but he highly valued the honour for he believed that with the Christian Endeavours lay the future of the churches. He would faithfully try to fulfil the duties and he would impress upon the members the great fact that without prayer nothing of a worthy character could be done. Mr. Milne then delivered an eloquent address on " Love for Christ," pointing out that service must be the outcome of a professed love for Christ, and that mere sentiment without work was useless. Mr. W. R. BESWICK read the annual report. Regret was expressed that Miss F. Mason and Miss Liddle, two ardent Endeavourers, who were the joint secretaries at the beginning of the year, had had to leave Warring- ton. The office of President had been filled by the Rev. F. Vaughan, whose work had been faithful, the reports received from the societies for the last half year having been far better than those for the first half of 1907. The Union Committee had laid down programmes for sarnest progressive work, and if the tide of circum- stances had seemed to impede their progress, they were nevertheless to be congratulated on their aims and achievements, which latter were valuable as an inspira- tion to their fellow endeavourers, and an uplift to their fellow men. Reference was made to a successful open- air evangelistic meeting in Septernber,and to the united missionary meeting in October. The work of the rank and file was prominently manifested in the praiseworthy effort to collect money for Dr. Barnardo's Homes, £24 18s, being raised. The Spring Convention was a very happy one, while at the Autumnal Convention the outstanding feature was the visit of Mr. R. WI. Davies, President of the British National C.E. Union, who gave them hearty and inspiring counsel. The Union at present comprised eleven societies enlisting 243 active and 122 associates members. Ninety-eight was the number of junior endeavourers shown on the last returns. This number was small, because the Earles- town Primitive Methodist Junior Endeavour, with fil members, had just withdrawn its affiliation, and the Bank Quay junior meetings had ceased for lack of workers. The returns for the last half-year showed increased activity amongst the Societies. There were more committees at work, and the reports of special work done spoke for themselves. There had been visits to other religious bodies, a " pound service "at Christ- mas to provide food for the poor, "sunshine "work, open-air meetings, gifts to home and foreign missions, correspondence with Indian Christian pastor, gifts to Endeavour shilling fund, and general assistance in Church work. One feature very apparent was the small number of associate in comparison with the num- ber of active members in some societies. It would be a good thing if the spirit and aim of the increase campaign could enter into these particular societies. They might give the campaign their prayerful consideration, after noting what other societies were doing to bring about a 10 per cent, or even larger increase. before the next annual Christian Endeavour Convention at Whitsuntide. There were six societies in the Union without any junior branch, and there were three Baptist churches in the locality, none of which had an Endeavour Society. Mr. Beswick made an appeal for support for the Endeavour penny fund which supplied the means, amongst other things, of contributing largely towards the extension of the work in the Indian empire and on the continent of Europe, as well as the maintenance of the various methods of Christian Endeavour extension which had been adopted in this country. The Christian Endeavour Watchword "Advance Endeavour "had not been lost sight of by the Warrington and District Endeavourers, and if they would try and keep it more steadfastly in view during the year, the secretary should have a splendid report to give twelve months hence. The PRESIDENT complimented Mr. Beswick upon the completeness of the report and said he was delighted the societies were doing such a mass of useful work. A consecration service was then conducted by the Rev. F. Vaughan. - _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>IS YOUR TROUBLE THE SAME?</head>
        <p>IS YOUR TROUBLE THE SAME?</p>
        <p>If it is your care can be the same. When describing to a representative his terrible suffering and his cure by Doan's pills,Nlrdames Cawley, 28, Lloyd-Street, Bank Quay, Warrington, said : " I started to suffer with my kidneys five years ago ; it came on in a most violent manner with pains in the small of my back. I Was unable to work for seven weeks. The pains were just like a stab from a knife in my back and between my shoulders, so that I could not turn in bed. The secretions of the kidneys were just ike the white of an egg and very painful, especially when passing. There was a sediment of gravel, and there was a frequent desire to pass the secretions. "1 had rheumatism in both arms, being so helpless that I could not lift anything, and had such a great weariness in the legs that I could not walk any distance without fatigue •, that was when I was up and about. I could scarcely dress myself in the mornings because of this tired feeling. I bad very bad headaches at times ;I could not hold my head up, and even my cap was a weight on my head. Giddiness was another trouble, and I was afraid I would fall. I was unable to sleep for more than two or three hours during the night and sometimes not at all. My appetite was very poor, what little I did eat seeming like lead in the stomach. Occasionally I had palpitation of the heart, and was so short of breath that the least exertion exhausted me. There were specks before my eyes and my sight was dim. "I was very susceptible to colds, which always flew to ray kidneys. At the best of times I could not with- out a great effort get up after stooping ; but when the worst attacks of backache struck me I would have to get to bed, and remain there a week. This occurred every few weeks. " The doctor win) treated me said it was muscular Rheumatism. I really thought I was incurable, the doctor saying the trouble would become chronic. I was always taking medicine and rubbing myself with liniments, but I cannot say that anything gave me relief. After each attack I got to work again as soon as I could, though I was not at all tit for it. Ido not think I averaged more than seven months of work during the year. " I read about Doan's pills and started to use them; I felt the first sign of relief in seven days ; I could eat and sleep better. Then I noticed that the urinary secretions were as clear as crystal, and the pains gradu- ally diminished until they disappeared altogether. A course of Doan's pills cured me absolutely. It is a pleasure to have good health. I never remember feeling so well as I do now ; and the only thing that troubles me is that I did not use these pills earlier. T can earnestly recommend them. (Signed) JAMES CAWLEY." Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellau Co.. 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Mr. Cawley was cured by Don's; be sure that you get " DOAN'S."</p>
      </div>
