      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>repentance zney may guess who have judged her character oorrectly. On their wedding-day, when the old man chaffed her—he could not resist it—she turned to him from her husband, and buttonholed him. "I won't be laughed at, daddy," she said. told you that if you invited that man down hem I should go out of the house. Well, I've kep my word. lam going now—with him. It serve* you right, sir. You've lost your daughter "—she kissed him—"but it shan't be for long, daddy We% be back in a month."</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES,</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES,</p>
        <p>ETC.</p>
        <p>"Heathenism Under the Searchlight: The Call of the Far,East," by William Rerufry Hunt, F.R.G.S., author of " Facts about China," Ste. With fore- word by Rev. William Durban, B.A. Price 3s. 6d. net (Morgan and Scott.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Durban says in his oreword "that "the devoted Missionary, Rev. William Remfry Hunt, is issuing Heathenism Under the Searchlight' at a psycho- logical moment. It is the opinion, emphatically expressed, of such supremely able experts as Sir Ernest Satow, Dr. Timothy Richard, the Rev. W. A. Cornaby, and Dr. Griffith John, that the Chinese people have come to the parting of the ways ; that they are emerging from the somnolent apathy in which they have been shrouded for ages receding to prehistoric times ; that they are for the first time displaying a I consciousness of the superior claims of Occidentalism Orientalism in knowledge, in science, in art, in wisdom ; that they are abdicating their time-honoured attitude of arrogant assumption, and 1 of their superiority to all nations outside of the Middle or Flowery Kingdom ; and that for the first time they are manifesting willing eagerness to accept lessons in all departments from the reptesentatives of modern civilization." Mr. Durban points to the fact that China has a population, in twenty provinces of no less than four hundred and twenty-six millions—which is "one quarter of the world's population in an area which is only one twelfth of the whole "—and he affirms that if China becomes Christian, all Asia must gravitate to the Cross of Christ." But he also points out, on the other hand, a fact which should have the greatest significance for Christendom, namely, that if" China should remain Pagan while adopting 'modern civilization,it must as a military power threaten the world as it did the Tartar host in the early Middle Ages." The object of Mr. Hunt's book, ovhich he par- sues in the most interesting and forcible manner, is to "convince any dispassionate reader that the time has come for a vast reinforcement of the missionary agency." The book has been written while on furlough at home, " under the shades of overhanging willows on lovely Windermere." The author, in his quiet retreat, has been able the better to discern and read "the signs of the times "in the vast field of his foreign service and he declares his conviction that "God is about to do a new thing in China." Any earnest believer in Foreign Missions who will read this book will be con- vinced that its writer is perfectly right in his con- viction. The book is very well and graphically illu- strated. It deserves a very wide circulation, and will certainly help the Missionary cause. "The Patmos Letters, applied to Modern Christen- dom," by I. L. Campbell, D.D., Cambridge, Massachusetts. (Morgan and Scott.) Price 4s, 6d. net.</p>
        <p>This volume of expositions of the letters to the Seven Eastern Churches, recorded in the early chapters of the Book of Revelation, is admirably illustrated by photo- gravures of the Isle of Patrnos, and of the sites of the seven churches. Ile writer lies bestowed much study on the writings upon which he comments, and has succeeded in setting forth their teaching with freshness and force. He prefixes to each of the letters an original translation, in which he claims very reasonablythst "certain distinctions are brought out that do hot appear in the versions with which we are familiar." The book is likely to be of considerable interest for devotional readers, and may be suggestive also for preachers who have not easy access to more abstruse and standard books. It is a book which would be a useful and accept- able presentation by one Christian friend to another,and should find without difficulty a fairly good circulation. " Man's Day," by Philip Mauro, Counsellor at Law, author of The World and its God." (Morgan and Scott.) Price 3s. 6d. This is a book which appeals to a very limited circle of readers, but to them, probably, it will give much food for thought and much religious enjoyment. It needs, however, a spiritual appetite, of the kind usually experienced by believers of the order of "The Brethren.. To others, it will hardly prove attractive or profitable. Its programme may be gathered from the headings of some of its chapters ; e.g. This Age and other Ages: The Coming Millennial Age : "he coming Age of Judg- ment : Scriptural Designations of this Present Age : The Divine Plan of the Ages : The work of the Six Days : . . The dispensational effect of the Cross : God's work in this Age : . . The spirit of the World: The " God of this Age " Bcc., &amp;c. The Book is well printed, which may help to its easier reading. A good deal of ingenuity is evidenced in the interpretations of scripture, and the book is full of that air of confident assurance concerning things that have been, things that are, and things that will be, which marks the teachings of The Brethren.</p>
        <p>Collins' Clear Type Press. We have pleasure in announcing further welcome publications from "Collins' Clear Type Press." Messrs. Collins are doing a most excellent work in bringing some of the best classics aud most popular fiction before the public in such an attractive and cheap form. We have just ritceived in the Illustrated Classics series copies of "The Ingoldsby Legends" with eight illustra- tions by T. H. Robinson, and "The Voyage of the Beagle" illustrated with photographs. In cloth these editions ale Is. and double the price with a leather binding, full gilt edge and gold autograph. The leather bound copies are most tastefully produced and worth a place on any bookshelf. In their series of 7d. editions of modern fiction Messrs. Collins have just published "The Happy Valley " by B. M. Croker, and " The New Arabian Nights" by R. L. Stevenson. The issue of Collins' • Handy Modern Fiction marks the greatest revolution the world of hooks has ever witnessed. It is now possible to obtain for a small sum area! library of the best books by the best authors of modern times. Since writing the above notice we have also received the following new issues from Messrs, Collins :—ln the " Illustrated Pocket Classics," Mrs. Browning's Poems, with eight illustrations by eminent artists " Windsor Castle,' with eight illustrations by H. E. Rutler ; "The Confessions of an Opium Eater," with illustra- tions by Willy- Pogany • in Collins' modern fiction, "The Deemster," by Hall Caine ; " Ensign Knightley." by A. E. W. Mason. Family Herald Press. This well-known publishing house has just issued "Love's Follies," a charming story in the Is. Gd. cloth- bound library edition, and also at 6d. in paper; and "For Her Own Sake "in the 3d. monthly magazine of fiction.</p>
        <p>Financial Reform Almanack and Year Book, 1908. Publication of the well-known Financial Reform Almanack—now with the added title Year Book—was delayed in order to secure the Board of Trade Returns up to December, 1907, but the book is now on sale at is. in paper and is. 6d, in cloth. This work is not in any sense a calendar, but is a com- pendium of official figures on questions relating to economical government, just taxation and free trade. The 1908 issue contains valuable and interesting tables of two hundred years' national income and expenditure, both for Great Britain and for 'lreland, from 1688 to 1870. (Taken from a Parliamentary Return now out of print.) With letterpress, giving sketch of the rise of the national debt also articles on Free Trade and Preference (with latest official figures of imports and exports, &amp;c.) : the sugar tax and sugar convention ; the. House of Lords ; taxation of land values v. socialism ; poll figures of General and Bye-Elections; and the usual statistics on subjects of general political interest WiNSTON CHURCHILL'S RIIINOCERO3 HUNT.</p>
        <p>The manner of killing a rhinoceros in the opens is crudely simple. it is thought well usually to select the neighbourhood of a good tree, where one can be found as the centre of the encounter. If no tree is available, you walk up as near as possible to him from any side but the windward, and then shoot him in the head or the heart. If von bit a vital spot, as sometimes happens, he falls. If you hh him anywhere else, ho charges blindly and furiously in your direction, and you shoot him sgain, or not, as the case may be. At such a range it is easy to hit so great a target, but the bull's-eye is small. I fired. The thud of a bullet which strikes with an impact of a ton and a quarter, tearing through hide and muscle and hone with the hideous energy of cordite, came hack distinctly. The large rhinoceros started, stumbled, turned directly towards the sound and the blow, and then bore straight down upon us in a peculiar trot, nearly as fast as a horse's gallop, with an activity surprising in so huge a beast, an instinct with unmistakable purpose. Great is the moral effect of a foe who advances. Everybody fired. Still the ponderous brute came on, as if he were invulnerable ; as if he were an engine, or some great steam barge impervious to bullets, insensible to pain or fear.</p>
        <p>There is time to reflect with some detachment that after all, we were the aggressors ; we it is who have forced the conflict by an unprovoked assault with murderous intent upon a peaceful herbivore ; that if there is such a thing as rihht and wrong between man and beast—and who shall say there is not ?—right is plainly An his side, before I perceive that; stunned and dazed by the frightful concussions of modern firearms, he has swerved sharp to the right, and is now moving across our front, broadside on, at the same swift trot. More firing, and as I reload someone says he is down, and I fire instead at his smaller companion, already some distance off upon the plain. But one rhinoceros hunt is like another, except in its details, and I will not weary the reader with the account of its new pursuit and death. Sunce it to say that, in all the elements of neurotic experience, such an encounter seems to me fully equal to half an hour's brisk skirmish at six or seven hundred yards—and with an important addition.</p>
        <p>In war there is a cause, there is duty, there is the hope of glory, for who can tell what may not be won before night? But here at the end is only a hide, a horn, and a carcass, over which the vultures have already begun to wheeI.—WINSTON CHURCHILL, M.P., in the "Strand Magazine," March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>IMPERIAL CRICKET TRIANGLE.</head>
        <p>THE IMPERIAL CRICKET TRIANGLE.</p>
        <p>It would be impoesible to conceive of a more inter- esting magazine for sportsmen of all classes than is provided lay the March issue of " Fry's Magazine," which is just to had. C. B. Fry writes in a most engrossing fashion upon "The Imperial Cricket Triangle," and presents every side of this much-dis- cussed question ;..F. G. Aflalo has an interesting and readable article on "In Defence of Wagers " ; Gilbert Jessop contributes an article on ' The Truth About Fielding" ; Guy Nickalls offers "Another Solution to the Problem of Fitness," and also writes an appreciative sketch of the late Edward nanlan, the world's greatest scalier; Henry Leach writes of "The Golf Temperament" ; Norman Wentworth of "Traditions of the Hunts " ; and A. Wallis Myers of" Lawn Tennis Under Cover " ; while an article of outstanding interest is that on " The Eye in Sport," by Et. A. M. C. The splendid budget of fiction includes "In Arizona," a thrilling story of life in the Wild West, by Cyrus Townsend Brady ; " A Chip of the Old Block," a rattling good story of a fight by A. G. Hales; "The Hireling," a fine hunting story, by H. Ramsay ; "Untrodden Ways," by Archibald Sullivan; "Sampson's Salmon," by Clifford Cordley ; and a most humorous story by Bertram Atkey, entitled "The Treasure Hunters."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DESERT PLANET.</head>
        <p>A DESERT PLANET.</p>
        <p>"It used to be thought that the dark blue-green patches on Mars were seas and oceans after the manner of our own," writes Professor Lowell in the March " Windsor.' " Generations of astronomers were brought up on these Martian SAB9. But as the planet came to be more closely examined, phenomena were observed which contradicted this idea. W. H. Pickering took the first step in revolutionizing the old theories, but even he stopped short of the truth ; for, while abolishing many of the Martia, he yet concluded that there were two seas on the planet. In 1891 and subsequently it was determined by observations at Flagstaff that there were no bodies of water there at all—that what had been taken for seas were in fact great tracts of vegetation whose hue and character changed with the varying seasons of the planet's year. "The discovery that there was no surface water on Mars was one of the fundamental factors in the build- ing up by the writer of his theory of Martian phenomena : for this fact proved Mars to be a desert world—five-eighths of its surface actual Sahara and the atter three-eighths but ill supplied with what is all essential to a world as we know it—moisture. Except in the spiing, and just .at the poles, there was no evidence even of mist or cloud to drop this essential fatness upon a famished land. Over the greater part of the planet and during the greater part of the year— about 99 per cent of the two taken together—the Martian sky remained perpetually clear." From an interesting article on " The Planet Mars" which appears in the March number of " The Windsor Magazine," a notably varied issue contlining another story, complete in the one number, in Sir Gilbert Parker's fascinating new series of episodes from con- temporary history in Canada, as well as the fourth exhilarating adventure in finance of Robert Barr's ingenuous young noblemen, Lord Stranlegh. It is some time time since any one figure of modern romance held the stage of the magazine world as dominatingly as this young man; and the present episode is as racy as its predecessors. Complete stories by authors of such varied accomplishment as Fled M. White, Frances Rivers, Christopher St. John, Orme Agnus and Jessie Pope make up a delightful budget of fiction, and the more instructive articles include lavishly illustrated papers on" Naval Training "and " The Exploration of Labrador." The fine art feature of the number, a study of the work of Mr. Fred Roe, includes some vivid pictures from history and literature, besides one of the best of recent portraits of his Majesty King Edward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>GREAT "BEER STRIKE."</head>
        <p>THE GREAT "BEER STRIKE."</p>
        <p>The' Wide World Magazine " for March makes its appearance in a new and striking coloured cover. The contents are quite up to the usual standard of this enterprising magazine. The first item which claims our attention is "The Man-eater of Malyah," a remarkable story concerning a tiger which accounted for no fewer than forty persons before it was finally killed. For sheer dramatic interest the final slaying of the tiger by the widow of his last victim would be hard to surpass. Another outstanding article is " Bensational Prison Escapes in Russia. written by a well-known Russian author, and describing some very remarkable incidents. In lighter vein comes "The Great Goldfields Beer Strike," describing a queer phase of Australian life— how the miners, thinking they were being overcharged for their refreshment, abstained from drinking beer altogether until the price had been loweted "Ten Days without Food," "A Ride for the Doctor," and "Twenty Thousand Dollars Reward ! " are titles of other stirring stories, while quite a number of other readable contributions make up an excellent issue. "WHAT WOULD YOU DO WITH £5 ?" "What Would You Do With £5 ?" is the title of a competition, the sum named being the prize, in the March" Captain," which is as bright and breezy from.. cover to cover as the month itself proverbially is. Filled with stories and articles dealing with sport and adventure, it is as good as a breath of fresh air. The contents include stories of the hunting field, the ocean, the University, and Far East, the West Indies, and the battlefield ; articles on public school football, photo- graphy, cycling, philately, and chess, hnmorous poetry and comic pictures, the whole comprising a number that it would be bard to beat ; and happy will be the boy (or girl) who gets it. CHRISTIAN FAITH AND DOCTRINE.</p>
        <p>This is an age of theological unrest, when the beliefs of former generations are on their trial, and the expressions of belief are being changed to suit fresh needs and conditions. For some time past the Publication Department of the National Free Church Council have been maturing plans for issuing a comprehensive series of volumes containing short re-statements of great doctrines. It was felt these would be especially valuable not merely to students but to the ordinary religious man who desires to find his way amid so many conflicting lights. The first volume in this new "Christian Faith and Doctrine" series is almost ready, and deals with one of the most important and difficult themes, " The Inspiration and Authority of the Holy' Scriptures." The author is Dr. Monro Gibson, the price 2s. 6d. net.</p>
        <p>CHASTELLAIN AND PHILIP DE COMINES. The Burgundian Court had produced another chronicler, whom it is perhaps little exaggeration to say" everyone" reads. Sir Walter read him, and gave us as the result the immortal romance of Quentin Durward of Glen-Houlakin. Had the manuscript of Chastellain, instead of the printed works of Philip de Comines, been in that " ruined turret of the library of the Marquis de Hantlien " I believe Quentin would have been even a finer fellow than be is. Now Philip do Comines had none of Chastellain's nlbleness of soul, none of his patriotism. He saw how things were going and, like a wise rat, he deserted the sinking ship of Burgundy, and " pasSed "into the service of Ring Louis of France. The world is the richer for Cornines' teachery ; for he and Louis were formed to understand each other. No doubt he belp.yl the architect of a far nobler ediflcethan Flanders could ever have grown into; but the French monarchy was built with very que3lion. able tools and materiels. Chastellain would never have</p>
        <p>stooped to cheat and lie, even for the most pitriotic of his native princes, as Comines stooped to do for hiS adopted sovereign.—From "A sterner Froissart," in the " Cornhill Magazine "for March.</p>
        <p>"BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON."</p>
        <p>A Letter to the Children.</p>
        <p>My dear Beautiful Warrington Children,—The other 'ay I was taking a walk just outside Warrington. The "Itt was shining brightly and overhead I heard a lark 'nging sweetly. The bright beautiful day seemed so full of the promise of Spring that I felt 1 wanted to ifig too and the words of an old Germ= song came lino Lily mind : J 'he dreary winter's going The fields grow green and bright, The balmy winds are blowing All sorrow from our sight. • The shoots and buds are swelling, The rippling brooks now run, The flowers their secrets telling Are turning to the sun." I noticed that the grass was growing greener and in the gardens, the pale snowdrop and the golden crocus Were clothing bare Mother Earth with their beauty. Do you know the charming legend which tells of the :)rigin of the snowdrop, "the morning star of flowers ?"' A curse had fallen upon the earth as the consequence of sin and as Eve wept outside the gates of the Garden 4:)f Eden, the snow fell silently, covering all things with its soft white mantle. An angel,beholding her distress, Fame to her, and catching a falling tear, breathed upon and lo ! it became a flower of wondrous grace and .‘,41-437. The snow ceased, a smile shone through the unhappy woman's tears, and his mission the angel departed; but where he had stood tlPspringing appeared a cluster of snowdrops like Ipeautiful pendant flakes of snow." Spring is the most wonderful season of the year. The earth is dull and dreary in winter, but as soon as the aun waves his fairy wand, the trees are clothed with verdure and the" stars of the earth " shine forth. We t ever really see the beginning of anything. In Spring' W!3fiee the result of a long series of wonderful processes, 'which have been wrought in the silence and darkness of nter.and largely beneath the surface of Mother Earth. cnigh the plants have been covered up by the the little workmen of the leaf and flower .747,8 been hard at work even since the sumrYter, Were only waiting for the Spring sunshine to --"lold the produce of their labours. This is strikingly shown in the bulbs. If you cut in two a rIliP or crocus you may easily see the future flower. ave you any pretty plants ready for the Bulb Show? have some snowdrops and crocuses out and I hope my 'ax7intlis and tulips will be ready. I suppose you are SeeiTtl; , in hear about the show. The Co-operative the "!tve kindly lent their hall n Sankey-street for liessin Er. The exhibition is to be opened by Miss c thildren ton on Saturday, March 21st at 2 p.m.; but exhibibitorertainment to an enat'" tlot admitted fill 3.30 p.m. when there will given by school children. All ririzes• are admitted free. There are numerous not bon h 's Year there are special prizes for bulbs drawin g t from the Society and for paintings and their „" C)f spring flowers :All exhibitors must give Hohres to the school flowers. by March 13th. to see you all at the show.—Yours ectionately, V. M. T.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>HIDDEN SKIN TROUBLES.</head>
        <p>HIDDEN SKIN TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>Pimples on the Back and Limbs.</p>
        <p>There are large-numbers of people going about at the present time, apparently in the best of health, but who, nevertheless, suffer secretly from some form of skin trouble.</p>
        <p>Apply Zam-Buk, whose socthing, healing, and antiseptic qualities are to be found in no ordinary or cheap salve, and whose action is quick and pure, subduing pain and inflammation, destroy- ing disease germs, and healing in a perfectly natural manner.</p>
        <p>Mr. A H. Lillierapp, of 97, Chewton Road, Pretoria Avenue, Waltliamstow, N.E., tells his story as follows :-" For eight weeks I was suffering with eruptions in my back, which came in the form of headless pimples. They spread until my back was completely covered, and the irritation was so intense as to prevent me sleep- ing. I tried several salves and emollients, but found no relief My back became so bad that I had to have my hands hound up at night to stop me scratching myself. I consulted a doctor, who said my back was the worst he had seen. He gave me medicine, arid lotion, but they seemed to irritate all the inf,re. One evening I read about Zarn-Thlik, so sent for a sample. I found such a difference after its use that I decided to give the balm a further trial. Mv back is now as smooth as I could wish it to be:7. Writing later Mr. Lillierapp says : —"lt is now four years ago last Christmas since I was cured by Zam-Buk. lam still wonderfully well. and have never since had to complain of the skin trouble. Zam-Buk seems to have given me an entirely new and healthy akin." Zam-Buk as an antiseptic healer and skin-cure is-unique, and differs totally fron- cheap ointments many of which do not possess or even claim to possess medicinal properties at all. Zam-Buk is the greatest antiseptic healer of modern times.</p>
      </div>
