      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>THE OUTLOOK OF THE DEBUTANTE</p>
        <p>One way and another the debuitante who comes out this spring should have a very good time, and there is a large bevy of fair young things waiting in excitement to take the longed-for step. One hears of a number of interesting girls who are to appear but of no special beauty. But then, that does not matter, declares the Toler. Beauties do not count for half so much as they did, and the interesting girl who knows how to be amusing and who has frank agreeable man- ners and a knowledge of things—and clothes— has a much better chance than the girl who de- pends (or rather mamma depends) upon a good complexion and hair for her everlasting success in the social world. The fact is we are no longer in the least imaginative and romantic in this direction, and no longer have any capacity for en- dowing a perfectly silent and unintelligent "miss" with all the virtues because she is pretty. The dull girl is acknowledged dull how- ever straight her nose, and large blue eyes are no longer called angelic by romantic but unobserv- ant young men; if they cannot twinkle at a joke there is no further use for them. TAB LATEST SAILOR HAT.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is our old friend the sailor hat in it latest and most up-to-date guise. This smart model' is carried out in fine black straw, and has a huge expanse of brim, with the slightest suspicion of downward curve, and a rather shallow round crowt. The edge of the brim is bound with a two-inch</p>
        <p>black satin, very soft in weave and not very bright in surface. Round the crown comes a band consisting of three strands of slightly-tarn. ished gold galon, plaited loosely together. The hat is completed by two immense choux on the left side, one of black and one of ivory satin. A. simple but modish little model, and one that is particularly becoming to a girlish face.</p>
        <p>ai , 8 trapping 01 ‘ ,ivot. _...4..41.: .4 4 f.: ~.4'; ~,,,4, , e.....0407,....4., ' 4.' m... 2 ' , I*--'11,'v.....-',,,. 1 , ~,. c.,......: .., ...„...,., \ ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TOILET ACCESSORIES.</head>
        <p>TOILET ACCESSORIES.</p>
        <p>The accessories of the toilet matter so much just now, writes our Paris correspondent, that they •are inclined to overshadow the larger and more important points, and a volume might be written upon them. Fascinating are such fal-lals as veils, fichus, handkerchiefs, jabots, chemi- settes, dm. Veils, long and draping, are being sold in every colour, edged with dainty lace frills, or with rows of fancy ribbon or velvet. There is an element of dignity and well-balanced grace in the draped veil, and though many women refuse to wear them, they carry with them an undoubted air of distinction and grace. Then there are some delightful fancies in the way of chemisettes, turn-over collars, and collar and cuffs sets of lace—Duchesse and Pointe d'lrlande. Smart and becoming, too, is the deep embroidered linen collar, while the new neck fit- ments in lawn or muslin, the top edges of which are furnished with tiny Elizabethan frills, and finished at base by a black or coloured tie, ob- viously modelled on tkat worn by a man in even- ing dross, bid fair to usurp the place of the hard up-standing collar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WAITING NINE YEARS FOR A NURSE TO DIE.</head>
        <p>WAITING NINE YEARS FOR A NURSE TO DIE.</p>
        <p>--- There is a singularly pathetic paragraph in the annual report of the Trained Nurses' An- nuity Fund for disabled nurses. After recording a successful drawing-room meeting, held by the kindness of Lord and Lady Ravensworth, the re- port says: "One annuitant has died during the year. The nurse who succeeds to the vacancy has been waiting as an 'approved candidate' for nine years. There are still nine 'approved can- didates' of seven years' standing waiting for vacancies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SIMPLE DRESSING – JACKET.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE DRESSING - JACKET.</p>
        <p>Very dainty but exceedingly simple is the charming jacket pictured in our sketch. This little garment may be made to do duty as a real dressing-jacket, as an invalid's bed-wrap, or as a breakfast-jacket on the mornings when one is just a little bit too hurried to eet into blouse and</p>
        <p>soriar. 1.. lßsus to az on nle moulders and to hang perfectly straight all round, no ex- tra fulness being provided by either tucks or gathers. Round the slightly cut-out and pointed</p>
        <p>neck comes a band of plain material, which is stitched all routid the edges and continued down the front. Similar bands finish the bottom of tho simple bell sleeves, which are made long enough to come half-way between elbow and wrist. The jacket is drawn into the figure at the waist by a ribbon belt, which is sewn to the middle of the back, drawn round to the front, and there tied into a bow with long hanging ends. Any pretty washing material would be quite suitable for this design, but the jacket is much prettier tarried out in a patterned fabric.</p>
        <p>THE CHILDRE.,N'EI CHARTER. The proposed Children's Bill, unless any very stfiking feature should be introduced into the finished detailed measure, is likely to be warmly welcomed by all political parties. Its outline is very practical, and it is a measure which shciuld appeal strongly to the women of the country, especially to those who, having children of their own, might naturally be expected to be inte- rested in the welfare of child life in general. It seems difficult, writes "Lady Phyllis" in the Bystander, to believe that, having reached our present state of civilisation, there should still be some social blunders unredressed which the text of this proposal reveals—notably that clause for the separation of child offenders from adult criminals. Truly, while we talk and wram,le over more or less abAract problems, the obvious is likely to be neglected before our very eyce. The times have changed since the great Earl of Shaftesbury had to fight against opposition to his bills alleviating the lot of the little ones. But it is none the less remarkable that in the great advance that has since been made many crying abuses have not received attention.</p>
