      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 29, 1908</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 29, 1908</p>
        <p>TO-DAVEI RECIPE.</p>
        <p>Qualm PUDDING. —One pint of fine bread- crumbs, a piece of butter the size of an egg rubbed in, a teacupful of fine sifted loaf sugar, the rind of one lemon grated, the yolks of four eggs, and a pint of milk. Mix these ingredients together in a pie-dish and bake in a quick oven until well set, but be careful not to let the pud- ding get leathery; it will only take a short time. When cool, spread a layer of apricot or straw- berry jam over the top. Whip the whites of the four ewes with a teacupful of sifted sturar. and either the juke of the lemon or a small tea- spoonful of essence of lemon into a very stiff froth, and throw lightly over, making it as rocky as possible, and piling it up high in the mitre. Very slightly brown it by putting it into the even for a few minutest</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RELIGION IN THE ARMY. Mr. A. J. M.P., and a Proposed Thanksgiving.</head>
        <p>RELIGION IN THE ARMY. Mr. A. J. M.P., and a Proposed Thanksgiving.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, Mr. Arnold- Forster (U—Croydon) put a question relating to a War Office circular, dated January 28, addressed to Lords Lieutenant and containing a suggestion by the War Secretary that a special parade of the Tesritorial Army should take plate on April 5 next for the purpose of giving thanks to Almighty God for the patriotic spirit which bad hitherto imbued the Yeomanry and Volun- teers, and of asking the blessing of God on the Terri- torial Force and the men who are to compose and direct it and of offering the prayers of the nation for the con- tinuance of tne blessings of peace. Had the circular, he asked, been followed by the issue of an order for the parade referred to. . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. King (L—Knutsferd), intervening, asked whether, considering that this country had no claim to. a monopoly of the favour of the Almighty and that the founder of our religion was conimonly spoken of as the Prince of Peace, it would not' be better to keep the name of the Almighty out of this question altogether? Mr. Haldane, answering Mr. King, was afraid that the point taken by him would lend to the exclusion of religion altogether from the army and navy.—(Hear, hear.) In answer to Mr. Arnold-Forster, he said : As the suggested church parade is a purely voluntary one, no order will be issued from the War Oflic'e on the subject. Although April 5 WAS proposed as a suitable date, the parade, if considered desirable by Associations and the commanders of units, may be held At such times and places as the various local conditions render advisable. The suggestion is that of the Chaplain General on behalf of the chaplains of all denominations, made with my full assent and approval, but as a suggestion merely. Complete liberty is left to Associations as to- whether they will adopt it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Shorthand Writers' Association.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Writers' Association.</p>
        <p>There was an excellent attendance of members and friends at the weekly meeting of this Association, held at the Winmarleigh Cafe, on Wednesday night, to hear Mr. H. M. Ashton, M.P.S., give a paper and readings from " Maik Twain." Mr. J. brown, F.I P.S. occupied the chair. Mr. Ashton explained that the apellation " Mark Twain " was the nom de plume of Samuel Langhorne Clemens, America's chief humorist, and a man of letters, and referred to the derivation of the title. A brief biographical sketch was given, showing that this great writer had been a man of many parts, and also that he was a man who, to the wide-world public, stood as a humorist of the first order, unexcelled, unparalleled. Amongst the books from which readings were given were " Life on the Mississipi," " Huckleberry Finn," and " Tom Sawyer " ; the versatile humorist's original way of looking at things, always thiough his own spectacles, the drollery of his ideas and the picturesqueness of his expression being portrayed by Mr. Ashton in a most uelightful style, and creating roars of laughter from the audience. The kindly and genial personality of Mark Twain,' apart from his writings, was also commented tipon ; and this sterling quality was well exemplified by the fact that he became personally responsible for the debts of a publishing firm, in which he was only a sleeping partner, to the amount of many thousands of pounds, and paid the obligation in full, although there was no legal obligation upon him to repay the creditors.— Several speakers at the conclusion of the lecture warmly complimented Mr. Ashton upon his paper, and it was agreed that it bad been excellent in every res- pect. - A hearty vote of thanks to the lecturer and Chairman concluded a very enjoyable and interesting evening.</p>
        <p>SOME STRIKINGLY SUCCESSFUL RECORDS. Nlakers of talking machine records have always recog- nized the difficulties in the way of securing perfect records of the soprano oice and of stringed instru- ments. It is evident, however, that all these difficul- ties have been overcome by that well-known firm, the Columbia Phonograph Company, General, to judge from some remarkable discs recorded respectively by Miss Ruth Vincent and the Casino Orchestra. Miss Vincent, who is such a favourite with all patrons of light opera, is under exclusive contract with the Columbia Company, and her records are truly. mar- vellous examples of soprano singing, revealing every shade of inflection throughout the extensive range of her singularly beautiful voice. The selections include some of the most popular of homely ballads as well as brilliant examples of the more florid style of song. They are all sold at the popular price of 4s. eech, 12-inch. The Casino, Orchestra is a patticularly well balanced and well-directed combination, and the Columbia records made by this musical organization are arrestingly realistic. The beautiful tone-quality of the violins and deeper strings is perfectly vivid, a bile the brass is rich and velvety and the wood-winds light and pure. The selections cover a wide range from merry dances to classic compositions. They are available on 10-inch discs at 2s. each and on 10-inch double-face discs at 3s. each. New numbers both by Miss Vincent sad by the Casino Orchestra are now being announced and the Columbia Phonograph Company will be very pleased to send full particulars in response 10 a postcard addressed to their " Department 20," 64 and 66, Oxford- street, London, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <p>tin RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SMITH OF THE CAR ABINEERS.</head>
        <p>SMITH OF THE CAR ABINEERS.</p>
        <p>BY FLORENCE WARDEN,</p>
        <p>Author of "Joan, the Citrate," "A Senaational Case," "A Spoilt Girl," "A Farm in the "The Howie on the Marsh," Ix.</p>
        <p>Perhaps it can hardly be said that she was a beauty, but there was something about Mary Gibson which caused most men to take a second look at her. . .</p>
        <p>She was a well-shaped, fresh-complexioned Kentish girl, with good teeth and laughing black eyes, just the sort of girl, in fact, for whose benefit a soldier draws himself up very straight, and passes with his jauntiest swagger. Trooper Smith, stationed with his regiment at the camp, not far from the village where Mary lived with her mother, had noticed her a good many times both when he was riding by with his troop in the morning and when he was off duty, strolling through the village in that particularly becoming uniform of black with the white stripe, which attracts the eyes even of the quiet girls behind their window curtains. .</p>
        <p>Mary Gibson's mother was a sailor's widow, and life was not too easy for her and her daughter. ' In the summer they let the best rooms of their cottage, which was a wooden one, in a good positibn in the High-street. But even with this help life was something of a struggle for them, and Mary Gibson was disposed to agree with her mother in feeling an inordinate respect for the pounds, shillings and pence, of which so few came in their way. So that when Mr. Giles Storey, who owned a mill a couple of miles away, and who was re- ported to have " 'undreds in the bank," sought an introduction to the Gibson, and paid Mary antiquated compliments in stately language, both mother and daughter swelled with pride. , "Mary," said Mrs. Gibson, solemnly, after one of these visits, "take my word for it, he's after you. Why should a rich man like him go to seek out poor folk like us if it wasn't thatthe's got his eye on you for a wife? Ah, my girl, you'll be able to hold up your head with the best of them, and have a trap to do your mar-; keting in, and never want for a silk dress a— Sundays, after all." Mary, however, though not displeased, took the magnificent prospect more calmly. " It's early clays to talk of traps and silk ilresses, mother,', said she, soberly, as she stood at the parlour window, changing the seed of the canary. "And he's no such great catch himself, for all he's got money. He's great if he's a day, and he's got a husky voice, and I never can tell where he's looking, with those eyes of his. If it isn't a, squint, it's next door to it." , Perhaps this contemptuous appraisement of the miller's locks was somewhat influenced by the fact that Trooper Smith, that dashing, blue- eyed soldier, was using the cottage railings to strike a match upon, and gazing ,in at Mary in such a way as to leave no doubt in what direc- tion he was looking. And having caught Mary's eye, he saluted politely. He had made Mary's acquaintance the Satut- day before, when she was doing her marketing, through the gentle mediation of their common friend the butcher's wife. Mary had not men- I tioned the incident to her mother, for Mrs. Gib- eon " couldn't a-bide soldiers," and had inculca- ted in her daughter a wise discretion towards I the Service. - .</p>
        <p>It was not, however, possible for Mary to re- fuse to acknowledge Trooper Smith's salute, and, slight as her movement of recognition was, Mrs. Gibson noticed it and was on the alert. "Who is that you're nodding to, Mary?" asked she, coming quickly to the window. "Not—.not that soldier fellow' surely ! " she went on, aghast, as her eyes met those of Smith, who was still smiling amiably from the, other side of the railings. But, to the scandal ' of Mrs. Gibson, the •" soldier fellow" saluted, her too. "My gracious, what assurance ! " exclaimed she, in horror, as she retreated from the window. But the poor woman was destined to receive another shock. A moment later there was a knock at the door, and Mary, intercepting her mother, ran to open it, and promptly admitted into the parlour the "soldier fellow" himself. It was all done so quickly that Mrs. Gibson had not recovered her breath before Smith was well into a carefully-prepared story concerning a friend in the Service who had known the late Mr. Gibson, and desired Smith to Said out the family and give them his respects. Whether the good lady was deceived by this brilliant piece of military strategy is unknown, but the three certainly spent a very pleasant hour together; and when he had left, Mrs. Gib- son, though she found fault with every detail of his appearance and manners, had to admit be. before retiring to rest that these "Army chaps," though they were without doubt heartless, de- ceitful, and wicked rascals, had a pleasant way with them. "But he mustn't come here again, Mary," she said, _warningly, to her daughter as they said good-night. It would never do for Mr. Storey to think we took up with soldiers, and them sort! "</p>
        <p>Mary agreed, but not warmly. She was not quite ready to take the soldierly vices for granted; and—and, well, there, where was the harm of having two strings to one's bow? In spite of Mrs. Gibson, the military strategist came again, and contrived also to time his strolls in the village so well that Mary, when she went out shopping, more often met him than not. She would sometimes casually mention these meet- ings to her mother, and sometimes they would escape mention; but what she did not mention was that Smith asked her to marry him, one evening outside the baker's shop, and that she told him frankly that she couldn't. Then there came out the story of the miller, and Smith raged, and indulged in a fine flow of military language, and told Mary that he would be lance-corporal shortly, and that, moreover, his regiment was expecting orders to start for the war, whore a man might have the luck ta get a commission any day. "Well, but there are other chances," said Mary, soberly. "I don't want to be a widow be- fore I've been many months a wife." "You'd be looked after, though, even then!" urged Smith, wistfully. The tears sprang suddenly to Mary's eyes. "Do Toe think that's all I care about?" said elle, indignantly. You were talking as if it was!" retorted Smith.</p>
        <p>"I didn't mean to," said Mary. Smith came a little nearer.</p>
        <p>"Don't you care a bit, Mary?" said he. "I won't care," said Mary, clenching her Sands stoutly. "Mr. Storey as good as pro. posed last night, and I as good as accepted him. I'm not going to play falee and loose with any man!"</p>
        <p>I "Except with me," said Smith. "Don't say that. I haven't," said Mary. "You've let me think you liked me better than any other man," protested the trooper. Mary caught her breath. If she had, she had not deceived him, she thought; but she was not going to say so. Mary bad learnt her lesson of life in a hard school; and there was more than the trap and the silk dret3s bound up in Giles Storey; there was her mother's', happiness and comfort as well as her own.</p>
        <p>" ~;ql,. and pray for you, as if you were my own brother," said Mary, with a little quiver in her voice. "Brother be hanged!" said Smith. "And if you're going to swear, Mr. Smith, I think I'll go. in.". _</p>
        <p>Very-well, Miss Gibson. I congratulate you. And I congratulate old Storey, who'll be ridin' to market snug and safe on his flour-bags while we're fightin' his battles .for lirm over sea."</p>
        <p>At that Mary felt it incumbent upon her te defend her fiance. "Mr Storey would know how to defend his country if he were called upon!" she cried "Aye, no doubt, Miss Gibson. And he's got fine round legs for a horse's flanks, too; and he'd make a first-rato gunner, for the enemy'd never know who he was firm' at!"</p>
        <p>And with this exchange of parting shots the separated, Mary remarking as she walked eivey that she expected Mr. Storey to tea. Now, the miller was nearer than she had been aware of' as she walked towards the cottage the saw him waiting for her at. the door. He was disgusted at the sight of his chosen wife in conversation with a soldier; and he expressed bis resentment with some warmth. Mary, who a minute before had been defeV- Ting him, now stood up for his unsuccessful rival. " He's a most respectable man, Mr. Storey," said she. "And I won't hear a word against one of the brave mon that'll be upholding the honour of our country in a mon:11 or two." She spoke with such a flash of enthusiasm as excited the miller's ire still more. "It's most odd," grumbled he, "how even the best of you womenfolk get taken with a sol- dier's coat! If he was in my clothes and me ill his. I should be run after just like him!" But he was wrong. Smith was as good-tem- pered as he was handsome, and Storey Watt neither the one nor the other. There was no help for it, though; Mary had got to marry the miller; and when, a week later, the Carabineers got orders to prepare for going out to the war, Mary felt glad that she had decided so wisely. "It't bad enough to thiuk of his having to be ahnt at now." .110 said to herself. crying. that</p>
        <p>night in her own room. " But it 1(I promised to zalrahim, it would have been a deal worse!" tough ho knew that he had lost his chance. Saiith eontinued to hover about the neighbour- hood of the cottage, and to meet Mary from time to time in the street. She VMS always kind, but a little bit distant, of course. Smith couldn't well complain of that. But when the last day of all came, and Mary saw a man in the brown kharki uniform that bore such a portentous meaning, lingering out- side the railing in the dusk of the evening, her heart gave a great leap, and she hurried out to the little wooden gate to speak to Smith for the last time. .</p>
        <p>"It's good-bye this time," said he, as he held out his hand. And somehow Mary thought that, handsome as he had always looked in his old uniform, with the smart little forage-cap set jauntily on his head, in the sober brown suit he looked handsomer still. "I suppose now you wouldn't go out of your way to walk down the street as far as the Parade with me?" Mary hesitated. It was, it must be the last time; her mother would scold; Storey, if he came to hear of it, would be very disagreeable; but she'd risk it.</p>
        <p>come—just for a minute," said she, dis- tantly. The dignity was only a very necessary protect- tion against herself, not against him. In a few seconds they were walking down the street, silently, side by aide. "It's just as well you chose as you did, I sup- pose, for your own comfort's sake, Mary," said Smith presently, using her Christian name with the frankest simplicity, though he had been strictly held to Miss Gibson" up to now. "But I want to ask you this: Will you put off marrying that chap till the war's over? Or till you lacer I'm done for, eh?" "I—l can't do that," said Mary. "You see I feel that confident," pursued Smith, robustly, "that I'll get a chance out there. And if I was to be in luck's way, you know, and get a commission, why, I could come back and make you a lady." Mary shook her head sagely, "You couldn't do that," said she.</p>
        <p>"And</p>
        <p>even- if you could, I shouldn't like it." She knew something of the ways of ladies, and she thought it must be dreadful to have nothing in particular to do, and to have all day long to do it in. "And, any way," she went on, firmly, "I'm not one to jilt a man when I've given my word." "Well," persisted Smith, bending down his handsome head to look into her face with those blue eyes that contrasted so unhappily with Giles Storey's, "will you just wait? No promise of anything else, mind, but just to wait?" " N-n-n 6," answered Mary, • reluctantly, steel- ing herself. "I couldn't promise that. Of course, bed wonder why, you know." "Well, look here, then, Mary," said Smith, stopping short at last in front of her, "if you won't do that, will you just give me .a kiss?" She was hotly indignant, of course. She de- clared that it was quite true, as her mother had said, that there was nothing like the impudence of soldiers. She meant it, too, most honestly, fervently. All the same, though, when they had walked the length of the Parade, and walked back again, and when he had taken her hand in both his down in the corner by the Coastguard Sta- tion, why, he got the kiss. And Mary went home with tears in her eyes, feeling guilty, not for having given Smith a kiss, which ought to have been Storey's, but for liaving refused it to him for so long, when he was going away, and might never, never come back. She didn't dare go up to the camp to see the Carabineers off in the morning, but ~her appear- ance was simply horrible when the miller dropped in to tea, in high good-humour that the regiment was gone. "It's remarkable," he observed to Mrs. Gib- son, when they were alone, "how sensible girls can be taken with a mere outside. I thank my stars, ma'am, that good-for-nothing popinjay's out of the way." Now, although Mary had not given her word to delay her marriage, she did contrive to do so; and although the war lasted some time, and she could get no exact details about her particular Smith, she behaved with great discretion, and showed little more excitement over the progress of events than anybody else did. It was not, indeed, until the first batch of wounded soldiers had been sent home that Mary heard anything of her old admirer. Then one evening Giles Storey came to see her in a state of ill-concealed elation, and remarked casually to her mother that there wouldn't be so many of the girls after Smith as there used to be. And he gave a looi at Mary, who went on with the making of the tea, and made no sign. It was two or three days after this that Mary was walking in the High-street, when she came upon a sight that made her heart jump. It was the wreck of Trooper Smith. He had lost an arm; he had lost an eye. Down one side of the once handsome face there was a hideous scar. It took Mary a moment to get breath; and Smith, more modest than in the old days, would have passed by. But she got back her self- possession, and Put out her hand. It sent a thrill to her heart that he had only the left to give, which he did awkwardly. "How d'ye do, Miss Gibson?" said he, in a painfully would-be jocular tone, and staggering as he spoke, so that for a moment she wondered whether he had been drinking. "You were right, you see. Ha! ha! No commission! No Victoria Cross! Only a scar or two to shew, and my discharge from the Army!" Mary gulped down something. But when she spoke, her voice was almost cold. You must come home to tea with us, Mr. Smith," said she. "Mother will be glad to see you, I'm sure." "No, thank you. Storey's there every day, I hear!" said Smith, mockingly. "But he'll be nice too," said Mary. "Trust him!" said the ex-trooper, shortly. "No, thank you. Not after what he's said about me—aye, and to me too—since I've been back." "Oh, but you must come," said Mary. And in the end the poor fellow, who was not in such good spirits that he could afford to hold out long against a friend, went sheepishly with her to the cottage. Mary entered the parlour first, and stood rather nervously in the doorway. "Mother," said she, in a tremulous voice, "I've brought a friend, a very particular friend, to see you.' Giles Storey, who was sitting by the fire, got up curiously, and peered behind her. Why, bless us. and who's that figure of fun?" was his amiable greeting to the man in the shadow outside. "Well, Mr. Storey," said Mary, drawing her- self up, and speaking in a full voice that came up straight from her heart. "I'm. sorry to have to say it to you, and I'd say it nicer if I could. But, begging your pardon, and asking you to look over my fickleness, it's the man I'm going to marry. I think," she sobbed out, apologeti- cally, "1 could have kept my word to you if he'd come back with the Victoria C roes. or a com- mission even. But—you see—l can't now ! " And if Mary Gibson was silly, remember this, there are plenty of women who at the end of a war will do the same.</p>
        <p>[THE ENDA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.—Under the auspices of the educational department of the Warrington Co-operative Society a lantern lecture on" The cultivation and manu- facture of C.W.S.scocoas " was given in the Wesleyan schoolroom on Thursday evening. Mr. G. T. Itashton (secretary of the Education Committee), occupied the chair and in a few well chosen words introduced the lecturer, Mr. It. Turner, C.W.S. of .\ Manchester. The lantern views showed the various) process 4 in the manufacture of the cocoa and the lecturer urged the members to buy their cocoa from the Society. At the conclusion of the lecture each member of the audience received a cup of cocoa, a sample of chocolate and a biscuit. The meeting was concluded with a short cinematograph exhibition which was highly appreciated. On leaving, each received a sample of cocoa.</p>
      </div>
