      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>MUSICAL EVENING</head>
        <p>MUSICAL EVENING</p>
        <p>AT MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S.BANK QUAY</p>
        <p>A pleasing feature of modern industrialism ,as exemplified in the larger workshops is the increaslng consideration which is being given to the amenities of life. Employers of labour are more and more tending to become also purveyors of amusement to those serving under them, and it is gratifying to note that the study and practice of music is taking foremost place in these changes. Privileged to attend a musical evening at Messrs. Crosfields' works on Thursday, one could not fail to be struck with the fact that,in this little colony of workpeople and office staff, there is evidently ample self-contained material for social amusement and edification. Under the capable direction and conductor- ship of Mr. P. H. Crossley a full-equipped and fairly well balanced orchestra discoursed a number of pieces in praiseworthy style. Having regard to the unequal executant capacity of many of the players it must inevitably happen that patches of the music in such a piece as the !Marlton&amp; Overture are beyond the orchestra's limitations. But theta was on the other band so much that was not only good and full of promise that one could not but put one's self on the side of the audience in warmly applauding all that was done. Of these the best was undoubtedly the " Hosamunde " Ballet Music in which the wood-wind, the brass, and the strings, vied with each other in bringing out the points of the telling passage 3 which are so evenly distributed amongst there. Mention should be made of a clever solo for piccolo and ' orchestra in which Mr. T. Gilbert took \the principal part. Then there was one vocalist, Miss Barnes, who in " When Alfred's friends" (German) song nicely and with a clear enunciation thereby earning a deserved encore. Miss Stewart,the 'cello soloist, was, of course, on importation, and her solos helped not a little to raise the status of the concert. What a hardship it was that- Miss Stewart (who also played in the orchestra) should have been under the necessity of three times adjusting her instrument to a piano dismally low in pitch end of none too good a quality of tone. In Mr. Moss the orchestra has not only a competent leader, but a violinist whose solo playing, as exemplified i a the one instance in the programme, was a demonstra- tion of good technique and artistic power of interpreta- tion. The" musical evening " reflects high credit upon the orchestral training which Mr. Crossley has been able to impart daring the short time he has had charge of Messrs. Crosflelds' forces. Besides officiating as conductor Mr. Crossley played the pianoforte accom- paniments with skill and taste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PERMANENT BENEFIT BUILDING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>PERMANENT BENEFIT BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the IVara-ngton Permanent Benefit Building Society wes held in Cairo-street school- room on Thursday evening. Mr. W. J. • haw (President) presided, and was supported by Messrs. Thomas Critcbley, John Cooper, S. W. Dillow, C. 1-1. Hihhert, R. Shackleton. F. Timperley (directors), A. J. Willett (solicitor), Edwin Bradshaw, and W. H. Woodcock (auditors), and A. H. Clive (secretary).</p>
        <p>TRH REPORT. In their forty-first annual report the Dircltors stated that the nett profit on the rear's working amounted to £7lO is. 9d., and the amount brought for- ward from last year's account was .£B7 199. 2d., making la total profit of .£797 lls. lid, for distribution, out of which the directors proposed to pay a dividehd of £4 per cent. (free of income tax), which would amount to £665 9s. 6d., leaving a balance of £132 2s. 5d., ont of which £5O was carried to Property Depreciation Account and the sum of £B2 2s. sd. forward to next year's accOvnt. Several sales of properties bad been affected during the year, and the loss on realisation of same, amounting to £55 's. Bd., hat' b n earth Ito Property Suspense Account, the total credit balance of this account now standing at £461 101. sd. The Directors took the opportunity of pointing out the security of the Society as an investment for further deposits and hoped the present shareholders would do their best to take up further shares and to introduce new members.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN pointed out that careful attention 1 had been paid by the directors and officials to the I detailed workings of the Society and no contract had ' been entered into without the strictest scruting. By working on those lines they had been able to place themselves an a fair and reasonable footing for securing a profitable return. Certain properties had been sold at a profit and others at a loss, and that emphasised I the value of the property suspension account, for if they had not been fortified with a fund of that kind ; the loss would-have had to he taken from the revenue and they would not have been able to pay 4 per cent. Mr. Shaw defi ded the policy of the directors in not , paying away all their money in dividend and bonus but I strengthening the sinking fund instead. The Society lATS now solvertt and every penny that they had som. ' thing to put in their pockets as well. The r 3option of the report was moved by Mr. Richard COOK and seconded by Mr. J. HRDOCOCK. ' Mr. FRANK SHAW offered certain criticisms. They I ht—l been told that if they had received a bonus they would have had to draw about £34 from the funds but he saw that the cash at banks amounted to £1,340. It would only take .C6llO to pay dividend and a shilling bonus. Many members would not have voted for the reduction of the value of their shares if they had thoaght they would be tied down to four percent. He suggested that the management should take steps to reduce the general expenses. The CHAIRMAN said the members were over £5OO hatter off now than when the present management took control.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY said that in realizing properties the Society might be decreasing the year's profit, but they were strengthening their position. Mr, W.H. WOODCOCK said it must be gratifying to the shareholders to have such a balance stu It submitted, especially after the ver v strict audit to which the figures bad been subjected. The members might be satisfied that their inveitments were perfectly safe and that the properties were earning the dividend which was being paid out. It would he a fatal policy because they had £1.300 in the hank to pay it all away when they had not earned it. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ELECT lONS.</head>
        <p>ELECT lONS.</p>
        <p>The retiring direct(iW(rdess-r-s. John Cooper, R. Shackleton and T. ince) were re-electd, as were also the treasurer. Mr. Aquila Cook ; auditors, Mess.... E. Bradshaw and W. H. Woodcock; surveyor, Mr. William Twies. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_____ A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>D NGERS QF MILK IN SUMMER.</p>
        <p>The milk problem in cities in summer isa really serious one, for you have to contend against three dangers, according to a writer in the World and His Wife: The danger of giving milk that is "on the turn"; the danger of giv- ing milk preserved with boracic acid; the injury done to milk by boiling and sterilising. Milk that has begun even ever so faintly to turn sour may set up, in a young child, severe and perhap's fatal diarrhcea, because this sour- ness is a sign that putrefactive change has com- menced in the milk, and this is continued and intensified in the child's intestines. Milk can be kept from souring for a considerable time by the addition of a preservative. - That most gene- rally employedi is boracic acid. Now this, use- ful as it s in many ways, is entirety unfit for a baby's stomach. Experiments have lately been made by feeding cats on milk preserved with boracic acid; and it was found that five out of six cats so fed developed kidney disease. Boil- ing and stdrilising kills the living cells of milk, destroys its entiscorbutic. properties, and turns its albumen into much the same consistence as the white of a hard-boiled egg, making the milk, therefore, much more difficult to digest. Babies fed on sterilised milk often develop scurvy and rickets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>NICE DISHES.</head>
        <p>NICE DISHES.</p>
        <p>LOBSTER CREAM. —Take the meat ot a small ..obster, put on one side a few of the best pieces lor garnishing, and mince the remainder. Mask the inside of a border mould with clear aspic jelly, and decorate it with strips of lobster, truffle half-moons, and lobster coral. Take half a pound of tomatoes, stew them well, and pass through sieve. Stir the pulp over the fire with an ounce of gelatine previously soaked in it little water. and add it to the lobster. Whip up one gill and a half of cream, dissolve a gill of clear aspic, and stir both into the lobster mixture. Season with salt and pepper and two spoonfuls of thick mayonnaise sauce. When well mixed set aside till cold, then fill the mould and set on ice till required. FISH SaLsn.—Take two cupfuls of cooked fish, half an ounce of horseradish and chopped cooked onion, a quarter of a slice of fresh cucumber, three cooked potatoes cut in small pieces. Sprinkle with a few drops of lemon juice, and pour over the following sauce. Serve with lettuce and tomatoes: Put the yolks of two raw eggs in a basin, pour in the oil very slowly, stirring gently at the same time till it thickens, then add two saltspoonfuls of salt, one spoonful' of mixed mustard, a good sprinkling of pepper, a dessertspoonful of sugar, a tablespoonful of tarragon vinegar, and two table- spoonfuls of thick cream. The ingredients must be added in the order in which they are written.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>PILES AND FISTULA.</head>
        <p>PILES AND FISTULA.</p>
