      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>TETE &amp;-e,X A M TNEK.</head>
        <p>TETE &amp;-e,X A M TNEK.</p>
        <p>FRISHUARY 29th, 1908,</p>
        <p>Mr. MCKENNA, in intro- THE EDUCATION ducing the Education Bill BILL. upon which the Government</p>
        <p>has for a second time staked its fortune, warned those who are the foremost advocates of the rights of Voluntary Schools that if the attempt which is now being made to settle the religious difficulty were not permitted to succeed it might be necessary to remove the controversy out of the schools once and for all. The principle upon the maintenance of which the Government embarked is the familiar one of public control for schools mlintained out of public money. That is a principle which at this stage no one denies. Bat it is evident from the attack which Mr. BArFouR delivered on the conclusion of Mr. McIcHNNA's speech, and which drew enthusiastic applause from the Opposition, that the peculiar variety of popular control which the Government proposes does not accord with the desires of the Unionists. Whether Liberal Churchmen or Roman Catholics who, are bound by Some political obligation to the Government will view the new proposals with equal distrust is yet to be seen. The Government proposal is comparatively simple. All schools which draw money from the rates will, under the bill, if it should become law, be placed under public control. In such schools no denominational teaching will be permitted, and no test of a religious character may be applied to the teachers, Mr. MqKENrre, although he'proii poses to abolish denominational schools as far as the local rates are concerned, does not propose to cut them off altogether from the advantages of the Treasury grant. If it is desired to maintain a denominational school a Treasury grant of 475. per child may be earned. But in order to obtain that money the school must be kept up to a standard of efficiency equal to that of the Council Schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. BALFOUR pointed out that the grievance in single-school areas would not be remedied by the new means. Nonconformists in the past have com- plained, as Mr. Iltryous admitted, perhaps a little tardily, that in single-school areas those desiring undenominational teaching could only send their children to the Church School. They had no</p>
        <p>alternative, and they felt the disability to obtain what their consciences demanded as a keen injustice. Mr. Bardronn claimed that in Mr. McKariNa's bill the grievance is not removed, but is transferred to other shoulders. Instead, he says, of the undenominationalist com- plaining that he is compelled to accept denomina- tional teaching for his children, it will be the :denominationalist complaining that Cowper- ' Templeism is thrust upon him and he can find no escape from it. Upon some other points the Bill makes an attempt to conciliate the denominations lists. Voluntary Schools transferred by the bill to the control of the educational authority may not be used for denominational teaching during school hours. But religious instruction may be given in them on Saturday or Sunday, or on any other day of the week after the ordinary school hours. Such instruction must not, however, be given by a teacher regularly employed in the school itself. These facilities for religions instruction will not, however, be permitted in any but single-school areas, since in areas better supplied religions instruction will, it is expected, be available in the Voluntary Schools which will still exist. It is obvious, from Athe indication we have given of the scope of tht. bill and of the nature of Mr. BALFOUR'S criticism, that the fierce tide of religious controversy which raged in 1906 will be repeated in the present year. All the ammunition stored up since Mr. BIRRIILL'S ill fated measure received its quietuS at the hands of the House of Lords will be brought out and fired with pitiles. persistency at the devoted head of Mr. MoKsztiva It is easy to condemn the violence of partisans on either side, but it must not be forgotten that those who speak most strongly upon this question are the really earnest sons either of the Church or of Non- conformity. Already there are signs that a small but hopeful body of opinion bent upon compromise has been created. It is nthinkable that the controversy should go on hopelessly as it has done since 1902. Eluctation has been retarded, progress of juvenile development has been neglected, while the theologians have raged. It is time an end was put to the miserable squabble. The solution of the problem does not perhaps dwell in Mr. MCKHNNA'S bill as it stands at this moment, but if a compromise is impossible then we see nothing for it but the secular solution of the whole distracting problem. These successive —and mostly ill-fated—Education Bills only serve to spread the conviction that no scheme of education is workable which does not entirely dis- sociate the State from the question of religious • instruction and leave the latter to be provided by the sects in their own time and at their own expense.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASQUITH'S Licensing</p>
        <p>THE Bill is essentially a Liberal LICENSING BILL. statesmanlike, and courageous measure. Of course, the Trade is up in arms, but this would be the case no matter how small an encroachment were made upon its privileges and endowments. There cannot be even an infinitesimal licensing reform without arousing the opposition of "the trade," and the outcry already being raised need not arouse any deep concern, It is easy to fling about such words, as "predatory," " confiscatory," " dishonest," and what not, as Mr. BALFOUR did on Thursday, but no amount of vehemence will make them applicable to the G werninent Licens- ing Bill. Certainly there is nothing predatory about a time limit of fourteen years. Lord PEEL advocated seven, and the BISHOP OF LONDON and other ecclesiastics and pillars of the Establishment would be satisfied with seven. The Govern- ment has doubled a period that many authori- ties on the licensing question considered to be ample. Mr. ASQUITH is wflling to serve for LEAH as well as for RACHEL. The Bill so ably expounded in the House of Commons on Thursday aims at whiat Mr. ASQUITH calls the two essentials to temperance reform. The first of these is the progressive reduction of licences, and the second is the recovery by the State of its property in licences so wantonly thrown away by the late Government. We are not sure that the second of these essentials should not be placed first. The Bill will restore the discretion Isitlren away from the licensing justices in 1904, and it provides for the reduction of existing licences by one-third during the next fourteen years. Mr. ASQUITH estimates the total reduc- tions at between 30,000 and 32,000 licences. The reduction is to take place in proportion to the population. The ratio in which reductions are to take place has been adopted from the minority report of Lord PECL'S Commission. Ultimately there will remain one pnblic-hone for every, 750 persons in Limilon and the large towns and one for every 400 persons in the country. The Bill also brings local option into operation. In the case of ell now licence a simple majority of ele,tors will decide whether within certain areas any new licences shall be granted, whether of the on or the off variety. A refusal to agree to the granting of any new licence could not be disturbed for three years, when there might he a new poll,' The result will no doubt be favourable to the cause of temperance, but not so predominantly so as might make it not worth the while of the owners of public-house property to co-operate with the Government in carrying out its scheme. The Bill has other valuable features, and if it is not a heroic measure it is certainly one of exceptional practic- ability.</p>
        <p>We are very pleased to A. TEMPERANC SI announce that the recent RALLY. meeting under the auspices of the United Kingdom Alliance, and perhaps the sitggestions contained in these columns, have already borne fruit which, while in the 'green stage at present, may very probably ripen into temperance work in Warring- ton of an aggressive nature. A meeting of people of all shades of opinion who are desirous of taking steps to combat the malign it.fluence of the Trade locally is to he called almost immediately &gt;Led steps taken to form a sort of Temperance Coun- cil with definite and determined objects in view. Now tLiat the Temperance Federation is dead so far as Warrington is concerned, it kiehoves all who !love sobriety and desire social reformation to corn I bine to raise the camite of temperance from the !lowly state into which it has fallen. Wo expect to be able to announce the particulars of the pre- liminary meeting in our next issue.</p>
        <p>Sir Thomas Whittaker has COMPENSATION AND always laid it down With LICENSING FIGURES. regard to the time limit, that it is useless merely to limit the operation of the compensation clauses of the Act of 1904. His case has been that at the end of a specified term of years all conceivable payment of compensation should end, and there- after the fill) monopoly value of the licence should be charged. We know now where the Government stands with regard to this matter. ,The general particulars of extinction of licences</p>
        <p>in 1906 are very much to the paint at this time. There was a total of 98,894 on and off licences ; and the compensation authorities refused reitewal in 1,575 Cases. The total amount realised in that year for compensation was,roughly £1,063,000, as against £1,136,000 in the year before. As to 614 extinctions £396,469 was paic' in compensation before the year was out, at MI average of £6BB for a full victualler's licence, or a general average on the 614 of £645. The number of licensed premises was about thirty-six to every ten thousand inhabitants, and the average expenditure per head in Euglaud on intoxicants was 24.</p>
        <p>Many controversial points CONTRACTING-OUT. will naturally be raiseci by</p>
        <p>the two greet Bills intro- duced by the Government this w ek. Probably the edneational measure is the one likeliest to provoke the widest diversity of opinion, and a point which we have already heard discussed seems to afford room for the most coufhetnig conclusions We refer to the contracting-out clause of Mr. McKerma's Bill. Two authorities on the Nonconformist side regard this as a distinctly retrograde step bicause they coote d that the large Voluntary !schools could be efficiently maintained on the 475. grant, could revert to private management, and COlid appoint their own teachers with whatever religious or other tests they desired, and thus cut themselves I clear away from all public control. On the other hand a highly-placed Anglican authority looks upon the measure as bitterly unjust because he argues that the voluntary schools coula not maintain the requisite standard of efficiency on the Government grant, but would, in the Case of a school of 1,000 children have to raise 10s. per head by voluntary effort, or transfer the school to the public authority and submit to Cowper- Temple teaching. It seems to us that the latter view is nearer to the truth, for it is obviously the intention of the Government to make the Voluntary school managers pay for their owii religious instruction. Everything practically depends on the standard of efficiency demanded by the Government, and it is hardly likely that there will be any slackening to meet tOe couvehi- erica of the Voluntary/nits. The point raised is certainly interesting and we should welcome any further expression of opinion from either the Church or Nonconformist side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>With charming appropriateness a vocalist at the Warrington Commercial Travellers' dinner on Satur- day evening sang " Blow, blow, thou winter wind" Needless to say, the appeal met with instant response.</p>
        <p>The report by Prof. Boyce on the samples of water taken from the taps of Parr Hall, and on samples of water from Appleton Reservoir, discloses no evidence of any pollution dangerous to health. The recent out- break of typhoid fever, therefore, still remains a mystery though we hear that a report on the subject has been prepared by the Medical Officer. * * Any rejoicing which may have been occasioned by the announcement in the "London Gazette" with regard to the widening of Warrington Bridge, in which it is stated that the Corporation intend to begin the work in two months, are quite premature. The statement is, we are informed, merely formal, as the Corporation in giving notice are bound to specify within what time the work will be commenced. In the meantime Sir George Nares, the Acting Con- servator, will hold an inquiry into the proposals of the Corporation and afterwards give his decision.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P. is putting forth strenuous efforts, at the request of the Warrington Corporation, to obtain an alteration of the Liverpool Corporation (General Powers Bill) which is at present before Parlia- ment. The measure is one of great importance to Warrington for by it the Liverpool Corporation pro- pose to nullify an agreement arrived at with Warrington in 1880 to ensure this town a supply of water from Vyrnwy if ever it should be required. The safeguard wss not obtained without considerable trouble and expense and we hope the Borough Member will succeed in his efforts to prevent the proposed interference with Warrington's rights in this direction.</p>
        <p>. * *. *</p>
        <p>Lt first blush it appeared as if the now Licensing Bill would reduce the number of licences in Warring- ton by about half, but on examinstion of the figures it hardly seems that the process of elimination-ITM be quite so drastic. Out of 201 licences in Warrington there are 118 on-licences to a population of 70,000. This equals 1 to every 593 inhabitants, against the provision of the Bill that the proportion shall be 011 the average in the towns, Ito 750. The proportion of all haves to the population of Warrington as estimated at the last oensus is 1 to 319, but it is only, the on-licences which are affected.</p>
        <p>* * " Consistent " Birmingham is buying Norwegian. stone for some new k public buildings, instead of getting it from Aberdeen, sacrificing its principles to save about £3,000. What are the Chamberlains about?</p>
        <p>* * * According to the current issue of the American " Library Journal," Mr. Carnegie's benefactions for libraries during 1907 amounted to a total of 1,631,630d015., as against 3,063,925d015. for 1900. The total amount now given by Mr. Carnegie for this purpose is upwards of 49,000,000d015. The 1907 gifts include 7,500d015. for a library in Fiji and 8,750d015. for a library in the Seychelles Islands: * *</p>
        <p>Blessings ever wait on virtuous deeds.-Cckurcre. The secret of success is constancy to purpose.-- D;sraeli. Between a woman's " yes " and "no "I would not venture to stick a pin. -eierrantes. Friend, beware of fair maidens ! When their tenderness begins, our servitude is near.- Vitor Hap.. We admire he man who knows, but we hate the man who knows he knows.-Pulitzer. The most faithful attachment in life is that of a woman in love -with herself.- Lord Lytton. Affection either grows or dies-unchangeable senti- ments are for feeble natures only. -Mrs Craigie. The blessed work of helping the world forward does not wait to be done by perfect men. - George Eliot. * * *</p>
        <p>The joys of a property owner : " Please, sir, Mr. Brown has sent word that his drawing-room chimney stack has been blown down and they are in a dreadful mess, so he thought he had better let you know at once ; and Mrs. Jones's little boy has come to say one of the trees has fallen across her front door and she can't get out, and she thinks her garden wall will fall down next, she says she call see it shaking." " Oh, very well ; can't be helped. Is there any- thing wrong at the back here ?" " No, sir, not yet ; only tho kitchen is full of smoke and smuts, and the greetthouse looks very shaky, as if it would blow in, and there are several panes of glass come down off the top." * * The intimation that Mr, James Glencairn Thomson, I only surviving grandson of the poet Burns, is likely to Ibe granted a Civil List pension has been received with much satisfaction by admirers of the bard. Mr. Thomson, who is in his eightieth year, has been in somewhat reduced circumstances for a considerable time. A number of influential gentlemen have interested themselves in him, and he will now be placed above the possibility of want. In this con- nexion it may he recalled that in July of last year Mrs. Sarah Burns Hutchinson and Miss Annie Beckett Burns, Cheltenham, were granted Civil List pensions of :.£.lOO each in consideration of the eminence of their grandfather.</p>
        <p>* * * The present Session of Parliament already gives promise of a fine " crop of bulls,"-the best sample so far being that of Lord Balfour of Burleigh, ' The noble lord shakes his bead, and lam very glad to hear it." But, if one may judge from past performances, there are many members of the Commons who may at any time he expected to " go one better " than " the noble Lord." It was Captain Craig who said, " The ti naked sword is drawn for the fight ; and never again ' will the black smoke of the Nationalists' tar-barrels drift on the Home Rule wind to darken the hearts of Englishmen," " The law relating to labour combina..t tions," said another member, " must be watertigh made so that no judge can drive his coach-and-tour through it." " Whenever the Prime Minister mentionS Home Rule he puts his foot into it up to the knee ' was the startling statement credited to a Unionis member ; against which we may place Mr. Mac Neill's " I will now put to the Attorney-General another question, which distinctly arises, Mr. Speaker, out o the answer the right hon. gentleman has not giveli," -</p>
        <p>- , • .‘ • , "."</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Notice. Owing to pressure on our space cansed by the new 'Government Bills this week we have been compelled to omit reports of the annual meeting of the Wanting- ton Society, the National Service League aebate, and the Holy Trinity Church tea meeting. Widnes Eisteddfod. At the Widnes Eisteddfod held on Thursday last, Master Charles Eckersley won the silver cup in the Senior Pianoforte Competition. This is the third silver cup he has won within twelve months fur pianoforte playing He is a pupil of Mr. Charles Henry Dawson, of 50, Saukey-street, Warrington. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDMt THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS ThT LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Presentation. During a conversazione held by the members of the St. Clement's Mothers' meeting Class,in the school- room, Bank-street, oil Thursday evening, a presenta- tion was made to Mrs. Barber, by Miss S. E. Bowden, , on behalf of the members, of a silver sardine dish, as a token of their appreciation of the good work she had accomplished. During the evening about 30 members partook of a hot-pot supper and several members of the Church Choir including Mr. J. Grey, Misses M. and N. Cross, L. and G. Ross, Mr. J. Davies and k, Master Stanley Bowden rendered songs. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., at Liverpool. On Saturday evening at the Victoria Restaurant in Liverpool, Mr. A. 11. Crosfield, M.P. entertained at dinner the staff and employ4s of the Liverpool branch oof Messrs. Joseph ,Cr saeld and Sons, Ltd. The' dinner was given in connection witn Mr. Crosfield's recent marriage —and the whole of the employes at the Liverpool Works were present. On Joining the party at dinner, Mr. Crosfield was greeted with loud . cheers, and complimentary speeches were delivered , by Messrs. Dolan, McGregor and Hamilton. Mr. • Crosfield spoke at some length on trade topics of great interest to his hearers, and apologised for the absence of Mrs. Crosfield who was recovering from an • attack of influenza. Later in the evening the party Droceeded to the Olympia Music Hall where seats Lad been reserved for them. Finest lit.Nrsa Burritrt, is. 31. lb. at ASEILEY'S.—Advt. ,IStockton Heath Tradesmen's Association. The first annual social in connection w;th this Association was held at the Hall Cafe on Thursday evening and proved a great success. The proceedings I took the form of a whist drive, dance and musical I entertainment and the attendance numbered about 100 The prize winners in the whist drive were : —ladies, 1, Mrs. Hayes; 2, Mrs. Worrell : gentlemen, 1, Mr. Caldwell; 2, Mr. Garside. The prizes were presented by the President Ole. A. E. Allen). The musical pro- gramme included pianoforte duets by Messrs. T. R. Ford and Pearce, pianoforte solo by Miss Hughes,songs by Miss Fanlkner, and humorous songs by Mr. Hayes. Mr. Ford played for dancing. The arrangements for the whist drive were in the hands of Mr. J. R. Wood and Mr. Klight, jua.,harl charge of the datice arrange- ments. Finest DANISK Burrea, Is. 3.1. lb. at ASHLEY'S.—..tdvt. `COAGULINE," KLINK,"TENASITINE.'—CemenIs ofr broken and other articles.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P. and Liverpool Corporation 8111 Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P. again objected to the Liverpool Corporation (General Powers) Bill on the second reading on Tuesday last, his notice of objection being put in as follows : —" That it be an instruction to the committee to strike out from the Bill so much of clause 16 as relates to the Corporation of the Borough of Warrington." The Bill will be set down again for second reading on Tuesday next when the Borough Member will press his objection. Under the Bill the Liverpool Corporation seeks to put an end to an agree- /tent made between Liverpool and Warrington Cor- , porationa in 1880, whereby Warrington, if it should , ever require it, could obtain a supply of water from ' 'Vyrnwy acqueduct at agreed rates. Clause 16 pro- vides that unless within three years the Warrington Corporation takes a supply from Liverpool the agree- : rnent shall come to an end. It cost the Corporation £l,OOO to obtain the agreement by opposing the Bill before the committee in 1880. LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and tronchial troubles. Nd. Of Chemists.—Advt ,cert at Ebenezer Church.</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>./ enjoyable concert, arranged by the Misses' 4toll, was given in Ebenezer Church on Monday subm- r;'g. An interesting and varied programme was the 3.- tted, the artistes being Miss Thornton's orchestra, (vioc:"Yric Prize Quartette, Misses J. and D. Upson t :..,10 duets), Miss Blanche Wallace, Miss M. ivri!jge, Miss D. Lockerbie and Miss A. Thornton, , ? la solos),Miss Alice Woodland,and lir.P.J.George `l3ongs), Misses N. Norman, D. Butterworth, E. Lister A. 13ottomley, (string quartette), Messrs. H. W. P. •1" L ok and P e and Misses A. Woodland and . , . J. Georg , }atrhurst (duets), Mr. T. Hughes, and the Misses. ! "-. Ashley, D. Harding, M. Brandreth and D. Collin 8/tl'ing quintette), Master J. Nurse (humorous songs), , aster Young (humorous song and stump speech). la the orchestral selectiOn " La Berceuse (Gounod) t, there was an effective flute and clarionette duet by a lb% Legh and Miss Connie Cook. All the artistes c. . quitted themselves very capably and the items were , cordially received. . -nest DANISH BurrEn, is. 3d. lb. at ASIILEY'S.—AdYt. '. Local Successes at Widnes Eisteddfod. . -, Local competitors achieved some notable successes at the Widnes Crown Eisteddfod on Thursday. The . tOCkton Heath Wesleyan Choral Union won the first , !ilcize of 6 guineas, with a silver crown for the con- in the &amp;lief choral competition. Five choirs entered hut only two—Sefton Park Vocal niotl and Stockton Heath—put in an appear- "nee. The test piece was "Ye Kingdoms and , Nations" (Ambrose] Lloyd) and the adjudicator spoke ;- l'e e ch,r37 highly of the winning choir's performance, t. z`erring specially to the clearness of attack and the ' . end and balance of tone. The expression was fine ` " °lid the • , thr otr gave an excellent rendering of the piece .'- 1-I,,i°llBlloUt. Mr. F. E. Peuketh conducted. The first ' ‘`Ze in the tenor solo competition ("Fairest maiden") twrbees awarded to Mr. A. Greenough of Warrington and • fAni:4l.Bt prize in the duet (" Kind and friendly " j --ng "), was won by Messrs. A. Greenough and P. ; w George of Warrington. Miss Violet Brown, of arrington, was first in the children's challenge solo t Contest, whilst in the ladies' challenge solo the first and : second prizes were divided by Miss L. Fairhurst, of . 'Warrington, and Miss Holmes. LINSEED CONiPOUND' for Condi and Colds. • Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists.—Advt. . Finest Dkiisa BurrEa, is. 3d. lb. at ASHLEY'S. —Advt. , Shorthand Writers' Association. . There was an excellent attendance of members and „. ..friends at the weekly meeting of this Association, ' held at the Winmarleigh Cafe, on Wednesday ; night, to hear Mr. H. M. Ashtou, M.P.S., give a paper and readings from " Maik Twain." Mr. G. J. ; Brown, F.I P.S. occupied the chair. Mr. Ashton explained that the apellation " Mark Twain " was the tOM de plume of Samuel Langhorne Clemens, ' America's chief humorist, and a man of letters, and referred to the derivation of the title. A brief biographical sketch was given, showing that this great • t writer had been a man of many parts, and also that be was a man who, to the wide-world public, stood as ;t a humorist of the first order, unexcelled, unparalleled. •'. Amongst the books from which readings were given ~ Were ' Life on the Mississip.,i " "Huckleberry Finn," •: and " Tom Sawyer " ; the versatile humorist's , Original way of looking at things, always through his 0 spectacles, the drollery of his ideas and the turesqueness of his expression being portrayed by 1 , C. Ashton in a most delightful style, and creating oars of laughter from the audience. The kindly and ternitilangs personality of ' Mark Twain,' apart from his was also commented upon ; and this sterling gnality was well exemplified by the fact that he became personally responsible for the debts of a Publishing firm, in which he was only a sleeping l'artnery to the amount of many thousands of pounds, ?°cl ?aid the obligation in full, although there was no . tegal obligation upon him to repay the creditors.— rferal speakers at the conclusion of the lecture itarmly complimented Mr. Ashton upon his paper, and , Was agreed that it had been excellent in every res- act.—. hearty vote of thanks to the lecturer and airman concluded a very enjoyable and interesting evening. Children's Hacking Coughs at night, Woods 1 Peppermint Cure. IR' 219.—Advt. i + The latest from " protected " America is that 65 . Per cent. of machinery in the woollen mills are idle, 0 and ti.. . all n 8 railway equipment yards at Philadelphia are :-... enest empty because there are no fresh orders for ar 1 ''cornotives. The Vulcan Foundry has got them, for . ...__ . .... 161It°wn, in Free Trade England !</p>
        <p>Personal. The Warrington friends of Mr. Arthur M. O'Brien will be interested to learn that he leaves England on March sth for Rangoon, having accepted a lengthy engagement as Chemist for the British Burtnah Oil Co. Theft of Tools at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday, James Done, of no settled abode, was sent to gaol for fourteen days for stealing joiner's tools, the property of Robert Osborne, of Fletcher-street. It appears the prosecutor lent p isoner the tools to complete some work Prisoner failed to return them, and it was sub• sequently found that they had been pledged. ilVarrington Bridge. Tuesday night's " London Gazette " contained a notice from the Corporation of Warrington of their intention to widen Warrington Bridge. The work will be commenced in two months' time, and it is notified that it will not exclude, impede, or interrupt the tidal or other waters of the River Mersey. LINSEED COMPOUND ' of -10 years' proven ecfacay for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.—Advt. • Borough Auditors. On Monday next the electors of Warrington will be called upon to select two borough auditors for the coming year. The present holders of the offices are Mr. E. England and Mr. Laurence Bolton who seek re-election, and a third candidate has entered the field in the person of Mr. W. Chrimes. The polling will take place at the Gymnasium and electors should bear in mind that they can only record their votes for one candidate. We hope the electors will see that there is no change in the auditorship this time. Messrs. England and Bolton have rendered excellent service and merit the confidence of the ratepayers. Stole the Knives. At the Borough Police Court on Monday Charles , Campbell, 11, Ship-yard, pleaded guilty to stealing two table knives, valued at Is., belonging to 1 Thomas Spiers, 4, Peter-street.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner went into Mr. Spiers' restaurant at 8 o'clock on Saturday night and was supplied with a Id. bowl of soup. Afterwards he was seen to take the two knives off the counter. When spoken to he denied having taken them, but a constable who was called found them in his pocket. —This was prisoner's ninth appearance, and he was fined 103. and costs (£.l Os. altogether), or a month's imprisonment. "()SRAM " ELECTRIC LAMPS.—Double the light at half the cost. See Windows.—Agent : J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, IVarrington.—Advt. Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes. The monthly meeting of the Council was held on Tuesday afternoon, Mr. John Barlow presiding. It was reported that 91,052 papers had been requisi- tioned for the forthcoming examinations, as compared with 85,722 in 1907 and 73,808 in 1900. The details</p>
