      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 29, 1908:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WOAEN AT VARIANCE.</p>
        <p>1 .1t the Runcorn Sessions on Monday, before Messrs. John Littler and W. Timmius, Annie Gayter, Mason- street, was summoned for having used abusive language towards Caroline Pendlebury, 92, Mason-street, on the 17th inst. Mr. 1-1. N. Livaker appeared for complainant and the defendant was represented by Mr. J. W. Knowles.—Mr. Linaker said that for some time defendant had made slanderous statements about his client. At the request of her husband she went to see defendant, end in answer to her question she said " You know very well all about it. You know it is quite trae. Everyone cannot lie." She also called her a bad name —The complainant gave evidence in support of this statement. Rachel Weedall, 42, Mason-street, also gave evidence.—Mr. Knowles said the talk about com- plainant was common talk, and defendant, who was a poor, weak woman, thrashed by her husband, was greatly distressed by it. She was sent for by com- plainant, and told her what she had heard, but did not use any bad language. Recalled, complainant said she sent for defendant, and the conversation took place in her own garden.—A tine of 2s. 6d. and 10s. 6d. costa was imposed. THE GREAT GALE.</p>
        <p>No street in Runcorn escaped damage during the terrific gale on Saturday, all were strewn with pipes, slates, and bricks. In Arthur-street only three chimney stacks were left standing. The higher grade school at Higher Runcorn, in course of construction for the Cheshire County Council at a cost of about £12,000, has sustained damage estimated at about £4OO. Principals, window frames, bricks, and glazed tiles have been swept down. In the Holloway a telephone pole was blown across the road, and about thirty wires were smashed. Warrington-road was blocked by three trees. Several chimney stacks crashed through roofs into kibhens and bedrooms, but no injuries are reported. No serious damage was sustained on the river or the Ship Canal. In Widnes and district, slates, glass, and tiles were strewn about the roadway. At the new Co-opera- tive Stores, near the Town Hall, seven huge plate-glass windows were blown in. The window stock was quickly flying about the streets, and many valuable articles were confiscated by the onlookers. Nearly every bit of property in the town suffered. Many roofs were shattered by falling chimney-pots. The whole of the hoardings on the east side of the Widnes football field wae demolished. The Transporter Bridge car was stopped for some considerable time. Flats coming from Liverpool to West Bank Dock had trying experiences. THEFT OF A FOOTBALL.—PARENTS' ..A.3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RESPONSIBILITY.</head>
        <p>RESPONSIBILITY.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court on Nlonday, before Councillor F. Neil and dr. J. B. Aitken, Joseph Wood- ward, 15, of John-street, was charged en remand with having stolen a football, value 55., the property of John Ryan, on the loth inst.—John Woodward and Emma Woodward, parents of the boy, were summoned for conducing to the comraission of the offence by neglecting the boy.—Superintendent Strickland said that Detective-Sergeant Davidson saw the prisoner and a boy named Ogden go up an entry behind Loney- street on Saturday night, the 15th inst. Ho followed them and found them in possession of a football. When they saw him coming they dropped it and ran away. He caught one and afterwards apprehended Woodward. The football was discovered to have been taken from the van of John Ryan at the Fairground. When charged Woodward said " Yes."—John Ryan said he saw the football safe on a hook in the van at roar o'clock on Saturday afternoon. At 7.30 it was shown to him by Detective-Sergeant Davidson. It was worth 59. Detective-Sergeant Davidson corroborated the state- ment of his superior. When he caught the boy Ogden, he said" Woodward 'pinched ' it out of the van on the market and we were going to hide it." When they were charged together with the theft at the Police Station Nood ward replied Yes." —The father of the boy asked Ryan why he did not keep his van locked.—Superintendent Strickland said that this was the fourth appearance of the boy on a charge of theft.—The Chairman said the Bench had con- sidered the case very carefully. It was quite plain that the parents of the boy had not exereissd proper control over him. The father would be fined 10s. and costs with the alternative of a month's imprisonment.— The father (indignantly) : You can't fine ma that ! I never saw the football. I never received any stolen articles. —The Chairman said the father would also ue bound over in the sam of £5 to be responsible for good behaviour of the lad for twelve months. They did not desire to record a Sonviction against the boy, and he would be discharged under the care of the Probation Officer for twelve rnonths.—The father: It is a bit off. —The mother : He only gets 265. a week.</p>
        <p>, of the application are as follows :—Languages and literature, 6,496 • commercial, 31,561 ; science, &amp;c., 8,806 ; handicrafts, 1,045 ; music, 355 ; domestic, 18,604 ; group courses : First year technical, 864 ; second year technical, 1,737 ; first year commercial, 1,039 ; second year commercial, 1,593 ; third year commercial, 895 ; fourth year commercial, 283 ; first year domestic, 1,023 ; second year domestic, 328. Junior Liberal Association. The weekly meeting of this Association was held at . Gregory's Cafe, on Monday evening, Mr. Ford : presiding. An address on "The taxation of land values " was given by Mr. Fox, secretary of the local , branch of the English League for the Taxation of I Land Values,who dealt with the subject in a lucid and !interesting way. He referred to the fact that only ; £700,000 was now being derived from the land tax but I by the scheme of the League £40,000,000 would be ! raised from that source. The League proposed a com- mencing tax of 4s. in the £ gradually working up to • 20s. and by this means land nationalisation would I ; ultimately be attained. The present tax was Zaased on Ithe valuation of the land made over 200 years ago but the League contended that the tax should be ; according to the present value.—A discussion followed, 1 Councillor Hawthorn, Messrs. J. Derbyshire, E I England, A. H. Rathbone and others taking part. I 1 Sale of Work at Lymm. 1 A sale of work in aid of the funds of Lymm ' Wesleyan Church was opened in the schools on ; Thursday afternoon by Mrs. Dodds, of Penketh. There j was a large gathering, many friends from Warrington ' being present, including the circuit stewards, Dr. Bennett and gr. R. H. Meldrum. Mr. J. Tayl tr, of Warrington, occupied the chair and after a compli- mentary reference to Mrs. Dodds, called upon her to open the sale. Mrs. Decide made an appropriate little speech, and was warmly thanked for her services. Ten children presented their cellectingepursee, the amount obtained being over £B. During the after- noon pianoforte selections were given by Miss Tilt Son and others. The stalls included a flower stall, adver- tisement stall, work stall, paper-flower stall, and refteshuaent buffet, and all were well patronized, the proceeds of the sale amounting to £139. REVOLUTION INELECTRIC LIGHTING.—" OsraM " Electric Lamps reduce your current Bill one MU. —Agent : J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, War- rington.—Advt. Operetta at Latchford. On Tuesday evening an entertainment of an inter- _ eating and amusing character,consisting of an operetta THE GALE. . , entitled " Golden Hair and Three Bears " and other In all parts of St. Helens damage was done by the items (dialogues etc.), was given by the children of great gale on Saturday. Hoardings, chimneys, and Christ Church School in the Parish Room. It would slates were blown down, and at several of the works be difficult to say which item or which performer portions of roof and exposed gables were demolished, deserves the most praise. The characters of the but then no sAlrito ow; a accidents er Rain-of important postal tel Three Bears were well sust G etined by Mr. A. Starkey, . Doncaster and E. Wright. May Higham as " Golden large tree telegraph lines and damaged tin electric tramway trolley pole. At Sutton a large piece of the Hair " was remarkably good. In the dialogue the Oft. stone wall around Sherley Park was blown into the part of gentleman was well taken by F. Shakeshaft road, and near Taylor Park several trees were uprooted, and the part of " Woodland Queen " was gracefully and large chimney-stacks wore blown over. Fortan- filled by Minnie Wells. The audience was most ately, the upper rooms of the houses were deserted, and appreciative,. judging from the laughter and applause, no one was injured. A most enjoyable three hours entertainment was TRAMCAR SPEED IN FOG. brought to a close by singing of the National Anthem. COLLISION WITH MOTOR-WAGGON. Great credit is due to all concerned Mis