      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S CUP-TIES.</p>
        <p>- - Will Warrington beat Halifax to-day in the first round for the Cup? That is the question of the hoar. Personally, I see no reason to have any doubt about the success of the Cup-holders, though there is plenty of confidence in Halifax because the latter beat War- rington in the final at Weaste in 1901. Still there is a difference between Weasto and Wilderspool, and I fancy the blue and white brigade will have rather more than they can manage this afternoon. In my recollection they have never won a cup-tie here, and precious few League matches—though they did once beat Warrington "A" by 5 points to 3. But that was a feat Little and Co. would rather not remember. There was once a great Cup-tie between Warrington and Halifax at Warrington, and the homesters won after being 8 points behind at the interval. And Halifax in the days of Archie Rigg, Fred Firth and Tootlaill were a greater farce than now. I think Warrington will triumph to-day, but they will have to leave nothing to chance. It must be hard, honest endeavour from start to finish.</p>
        <p>' The Warrington team was selected on Thursday night and will, in the main, I think give satisfaction. It is unfortunate that men like Lees, Jenkins, and the two Boardmans are not available, but the side should be little the worse: Lloyd is the weak spot on paper but this young player is improving and I am hoping that he will distinguish himself in a totally different fashion to day from what he did in a first-round tie at Batley two years ago. Last Saturday, he played a smart game, and if the forwards do well again I think he will not be found wanting. At three-quarter, Taylor has been given the preference over Isherwood on the ground of fitness, and Dickenson will play left centre, the position which suits him bast. It is some- what of a surprise to find Heath among the forwards again,but the old Salford man though slow in the open has some good points as a cup-tie scrummager. Watkin will have his first experience of cup-tie foot- ball, but alongside Taylor he should do well.</p>
        <p>The team is : Tilley, fall back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and W akin, three-quarters ; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs ; Thomas, Shugars, Jenkins, Belton, Heath and Naylor, forwards.</p>
        <p>A Halifax c Trespondent writes me as followa : " The Halifax team has not yet been definitely selected, only to the extent of choosing what is known as the Cup-tie seventeen. It is usual with the Halifax club to leave the final selection until the eleventh hoar, and then build up the team according to the conditions. The following players are down in. black and white from which to pick the team: -Little, full back; Dai Thomas, Joe Riley, Ward, Eccles, and W. J. Williams, three-quarters ; T. H. Grey and Hilton, half backs ; Brearley, Hammond, Langhorn, Little- wood, Morley, Mallinson, Proctor, Robinson, and Sunderland, forwards. "It is a long time since the Halifax club had so many first team players available. For instance W. J. Williams has just returned to the field after being a long time injured. With such star wings as Williams, Eccles and Dal Thomas, the committee will be in rather a fix as to the final choice. Hilton and T. H. Grey hare performed consistently together, and Hilton particularly takes a, lot of watching. The Halifax forwards have not beeji p trticularly happy in , their displays this season. They are not anything like as powerful in that department as when Halifax defeated Warrington in the final at Weaste. If preparation counts for anything the Halifax men will prove a very stern foe, for they have practically lived on the training ground during the past week. " If the Halifax club are to make a decent financial show at the 'end of the season, the team must do well in the Cup-ties. That fiat has gone forth. If they lose at Warrington there will be no profit on this season's workings. Hence the blue and white brigade should turn out in the pink of condition at Wilderspool, full of determination to strive their utmost to carve their say to victory, conscious of the heavy resposibility imposed upon them. A. lot will depend uppn the forwards. If they can hold their own, it is believed in Halifax that the blue and white , backs can take care of themselves, arld it is not alto- gether blind faith which prompts the belief Halifax way that Warrington will receive their quietus in the first round."</p>
        <p>The other first-round matches are as tollows: Huddersfield v. Broughton Rangers. Whitehaven v. 'St. Helens. Dewsbury v. Oldham. Castleford Halfacre v. York. Salford v. Widnes. Barrow St. George's v. Batley. Runcorn v. Keighley. Swinton v. Hull. Leigh v. Bradford. Wigan Hightleld v. Bramley. Wakefield Trinity v. Hull Kingston Rovers. Wigan v. Rochdale Hornets. Beverley v. Merthyr Tydvil. 13arrorr v. Mißom.</p>
        <p>With all the interest now centred in the cup, there is no need to say much about the Wigan match last Saturday. Warrington certainly rose to the occasion and played is their best and most dashing form, the previous week's defeat of 11-5 being wiped out by 14-2. Wigan got a surprise thrashing, and were clearly the inferior side all through. Leytham for once in a way was rendered impotent, and Watkio did well to score his first try for Warrington against the till England left wing. Fish too played a spark- ling game on the other side, and the forwards fairly rose to the occasion.</p>
        <p>The result of the match did not affect the West Lancashire Championship which Wigan had already won, but it may cost the Central Park team their fourth place in the League.</p>
        <p>The South West Lancashire League table up-to date is as follows :</p>
        <p>Points. 14 P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts Wigan Runcorn lO G . 4 0.. 79 65 .12 Warrington Leigh St. Helens 8 ", 5 1 .41 121 5 Widnes oritt</p>
        <p>The Leave table up-to-date is as follows: For. Apt. Per- P. W. L 11 G. T. P GTP eVge</p>
        <p>Oldham liunslot —2s 19 5 1 72 GO 324 31 42 188 78'00</p>
        <p>Salford Broughton Wigan 26 17 8 1 56 96 400 32 27 175 6730 Batley 26 17 9 050 62 286 36 50 222 6518 Hull K. R. Keighley 25 15 10 0 49 55 263 47 44 226 60.00 Halifax. Warrington Bradford 25 14 11 0 41 51 244 33 66 261 56.00 Wakefield T._ 26 14 11 1 53 77 337 50 60 280 5576 Barrow Hull 27 14 13 0 49 66 296 36 44 204 5185 Runcorn 24 12 12 0 22 53 203 19 38 152 5000 Dewsbury 2l 10 13 1 40 47 221 36 56 240 4375 Rochdale I Leeds Huddersfield Iklerthyr Leigh.., 92 7 15 0 21 42 168 41 61 280 31.81 Swinton York 23 7 16 037 42 200 58 65 311 30•43 St. -Helens 2l 6 16 2 28 34 158 43 95 371 291.6 Ebbw Vale 2l 5 15 1 15 24 102 '39 63 267 2618 Widnes 22 3 15 4 16 34 134 38 63 265 2272 Bromley</p>
        <p>-- Isherwood's I:netit inateh hAq requited in the hand some sum of £165 ss. 4d. being re'alised —and a cheque for this amount was handed to burn at the Committee meeting on Thursday evening.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday next Warrington play a League match with Bradford at Wilderspoo[, kick-off at 4.30.</p>
        <p>DEA.Tif OF AltS. LONGTON. A. large circle of people .viii hoar with regret of the death of :qrs. longton. which to place at her resi- dence, The Alders, Cross-Isne, girlestown, on Saturday morning,. She .cai 71 veirs of age ani is the nother of Mr. if. J. Lun4ton, I,Verrington.</p>
