      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by .John Workman:.</p>
        <p>M.S.S. must bew addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week. Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on onside of the paper</p>
        <p>only, an.it enitearour to .make their letters.. cind</p>
        <p>not.is. short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the reit name. of the .writer should be</p>
        <p>forivaraed in confidence to tit:6 Editor of the column. TEMPERANCE REFORM.</p>
        <p>All who have read the Editor's leaderette on ." A Temperance Rally," and the report of the big meeting held in the Parr H dl, must have been impressed with the statements, and set thinking of the necessity for combined action to fight against the curse of intem- perance. Mr. James Dodds is to be congrAtulated, and thanked, for his efforts in endeavouring to arouse public opinion to a pitch of earnest aggressiveness. May he succeed beyond his anticipations. Now, it would be presumptuous in me, who have never had to attend at the Police Courts and listen to the cases that cdntinnally crop up, to say how much drink is to be held responsible for the unhappy homes that are in Warrington. I can, however, speak of the blame that exist and of the contaminated atmosphere in which little children are being reared. I can also say how much I deplore the excessive drinking which 14 SO prevalent amongst the young people of our town (to say nothing of those who have reached middle age). What is to be done to save the town from the curse of drunkenne is ?</p>
        <p>It always appears to be such a difficult task to approach this question without hurting the feelings of members of the Established Church, the Conserva- tive Party and the Brewing fraternity. They all seem to look upon any criticism of drunkenness as an encroachment upon their peculiar preserves, and so long as they will take upon themselves the responsi- bility. so long, t take it, must they bear the brunt of the criticism. Instead of objecting to Temperance Reform, however, they ought to welcome it with both bands and work earnestly and long for its consummation.</p>
        <p>All must be anxiously waiting to see what line of action is to be adopted. Nothing short of a practical forward scheme can hope to be successful. JOEIN WORKIIA.N. THE LA.BOUR PARTY AND TEIE SOCIALT.STS.</p>
        <p>Since the formati m of the Labour Party many attempts have been made to create discussion and dis- ruption between the trades union section and the Socialists. Mstnhers of the Liberal and Tory parties have appealed to trades unionists to sever their con- nection with the party, or to expel Socialists. Mr. F. Maddison, M.P., has quite recently been appealing to the trades unionists, and especially to Mr. Shackleton, M.P, to dissolve the alliance. Another Liberal, Mr. H. Vivian, M.P., also Sir Henry Seton Carr and Mr. Jovnson Hicks of the Tory party, have been trying to disrupt the Labour party. These men and others I could name would object to a Labour party, acting independent of the other parties, even though no Socialists were affiliated thereto.</p>
        <p>One of the chief arguments against the alliance is that the funds of the trades unionists are being exploited by the Socialists to further their own principles. It is repeatedly stated " that only a small proportion of the income of the Labour narty is con- tributed by the Socialists, and that the Socialist mem- bers of Parliament receive £1,409 per annum from the Parliamentary fund." Our opponents who argue from that standpoint ignore the fact (intentionally no doubt) that there are thousands of Socialists Who are trades unionists, and who are contributing to the funds of the Labour party both as trades unionists and Socialists. The work of the Independent Labour Party during the past four- teen years should be taken into consideration. Hundreds of thousands of public meetings have been held, and pamphlets and leaflets have been circulated by the million, at a coet of thousands of pounds, con- tributed almost entirely by the members of that organization. By these and other methods, they have been educating the public in the principles of inde- penlegt labour repreientation and S icialistn. It is quite safe to say, that had it not been for the work done by the Independent Libour Party there would have been no Labour party such as we 'find t Before many more weeks have pissed, the 760 branches of the Independent Labour Party will be holding froin 1,500 to 2,000 meetings each week. Surely the work which has been done, and is now being done by the members oc that organization, at such a sacrifice of time and expense, ought to count for something, if comparisons are to be made. It is unfair to argue that the Socialists should only receive direct representation or support in proportion to the sum contributed by them to the Labour Party. Is there anyone who is prepared to say that the Socialist Members of Parliament have not been as loyal to trades union princioles whilst in the House of Commons as any of the trades union Mimbers ? . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Another fact xv.' hich should be remsubered is that it was at the invitation of the trades unionists the Socialists became members of the Lab or party, the ro- fore it is unfair to sugglst uo‘v that thei be expelled simply b3C31190 Socialism has gyr ,was, rapidly. It is the Socialiet elemlut in the Labaur party which provides the driving force, and supplies it an ideal. It is a well-known fact that the bulk of the active trades unionists are Socialists</p>
        <p>Those who are protesting mast 'against what they call 'the exploitation of trades union fuads " are those who have always been oppased to a Lib:my party acting indepeadeatly of Tory and Libermi p irties. In conclusion, permit me to say that in my opinion a Labaur party without its Soc;alistn would nit be worth the name of a Labour party. If the Socialists were expelled from this party the trades unionists would be the greatest losers. Such is the opinioa of one who went to Hull as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LICENSING BILL.</head>
        <p>THE LICENSING BILL.</p>
        <p>COMPULEOAY REDUCTION OF</p>
        <p>LICENCES.</p>
        <p>Valuable Temperance Reforms</p>
        <p>If r. Asquith introduced his Licensing Bill to a full House of Commons on Thursday. The following are the chief points in the Bill : _</p>
        <p>A time !Irak of 14 years on compensation. Within that time the number of licences to be reduced throughout the country to numbers, fixed according to density of population, and ranging from one for 400 people in the country to one to 1,000 people in poor and crowded quarters of the towns. Total estimated reduction 32,000. A central licensing commission to be appointed to approve schemes —which local authorities MUST draw up—and control funds. Power to refuse to issue any new licence given to majority of people in the locality. Compensation for the loss of licences to be paid out of a fund levied as at present ou the trade on a reduced scale. No more appeals from Town Benches to Quarter Sessions. No children allowed in bars.</p>
        <p>I All clubs to be registered annually. Chief constables or other high officials (net in uniform) may inspect any club, in Pall Mall or elsewhere. Local Option indicated at the end of the time limit. Sunday opening in country limited to one hoar mid- day—two hours evening. Bona-fide travellers to go six instead of three miles Mr. Dodds' Views. Mr. Dodds. who was chairman •f the United Kingdom Alliance meeting in Warrington last week writes to say that :</p>
        <p>The Bill as one which ought to be supported by Temperance reformers. It is a drastic measure, based on fundamental principles. No doubt a shorter time limit would have been preferable, as well as an earlier resort to local option in resoect of existing licences. These, however, are to be reduced within 14 years to numbers fixed according to density of population, and ranging from one house for every 409 people in rural districts to one per 1,000 people in the crowded parts of great towns. It is estimated that, within the time named, a reduction of 30,000 licencss will result ; this diminution of the number of public-nooses will be a great gain. In regard to new licences, the principal of veto is to come at once into play. This is us insignificant a I :antage : it will continually afford op p irtnnities for the expression of local sentiment, and be a source of po.ver which will be more and more valued and utilized as the results of its use begin to show themselves and develop. The equally stringent treatment of all clubs, the stoppage of children's visits to bars, and of appeals to Quarter Sessions, together with the shorter openings on Stindays, a,e all admirable provisions; while, in regard to the payment of compensation for loss of licences. the Government, endorsing the policy of Mr. Balfour's Act of 1901, levies this upon " the trade." There is less objection to compensation, so long as it comes from those who retain the licences and their enhanced values. As regards shareholders and debenture holders in " the trade," they cannot reason- ably complain ; they knew, or ought to have known, when they invested their money the risky character of such investments ; in any case, self interest must not be allowed to stand in the way of the public weal.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., !fr. Guest writes have not sufficiently considered the Licensing Bill to be able to say much about it. It seems a brave attempt on the Dart of the Government to deal with a difficult and thorny question, and will have the support of all Temperance Reformers who are willing to take as meth as they can get. Whatever view is taken of alcohol, everyone who wishes well to the country, must admit that the present facilities for drinking are much greater than they ought to be. The temperance party thought 10 years limit sufficient ! but will I think be willing to allow 14 years as proposed in the Bill. The restoration of peWer to the magistrates to deal with licences is a move in the right direction. Sunday closing is needed and it is to be regretted that this will not at present apply te London. The suppres- sion of 30.000 licences during the 14 years will do good, and seeing that the compensation fund is to he put on a better basis, no injustice will be done. It is only right and proper that clubs should come into line with other licensed houses and the Bill puts them under proper control. Children under 16 years ought not to be served in a publie-honse, and. the Bill deals with this question. Local option in regard to new licences should certainly be granted. It is a gross injustice to a nem neigboourhood that a licence taken from a congested area should be foisted 'on to them without the consent of the inhabitants: It is to be hoped that local option will become general, and that comparatively small areas will ultimately have the power vested in the people to say what licences they will or will not have in their midst. ,This Bill will be fought by the trade, but the Government will be backed by all the forces working for righteousness. it is an unfortunate thing that the trade cannot rellise hom mach mischief is done by etisting facilities for drinking. If the good of the community was considered more, they would see that much might be done by themselves to lessen the evil, hut as they will not move in the matter the Government is compelled to act, and the cry of confiscation. robbery, &amp;c., is a bogey. The monopoly granted to the trade has been a financial gift of untold value. The Bill of 1901 gave something like a vested interest in this gift. This has made the work of the present Government much more difficult and I think the trade should see that their rights have been con- sidered in a most fair and generous manner. If all who have helm injnred by the trade could he treated with the nale consideration and compensation for the loss they sustained he granted, I fear the trade would have more came to complain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
