le of produc- ing effects that could not be secured naturally. It will certainly vary according to different tetripsrarneut or environmennt but every adult at one time or another yields to it in a greater or less degree. Therefore I claim that, under certain conditions, reasonable facilities should be provided for the supply of alcoholic liquors to those who require them. A teetotaller has no more moral right to prohibit the legitimate supply of intoxicants to a moderate drinker than has an atheist to forbid his religious neighbour to periodically attend a place of worship. The aim of all 'true temperance reformers should be not to create a " professedly " teetotal country hut to gradually remove from our midst the paralysing influence of excessive drinking. Let us entirely abandon the futile medireval notion . of transforming this land into a childishly over- squeamish puritanical haven and place in its stead the !lgvii:t' .--;CF,tlr, WARRINGTO4 far nobler and more statesmanlike conception of a country inhabitated by a populace composed of free courageous self-reliant fearless men and women. DEMOS.</p>
