        <p>, . • . , the delegates from the local group being Messrs. W. H. Woodcock and E G. Burgess. It was decided to form the federation and that meetings be held once every three months in different towns in the district. The first meeting (to take the form of a business meeting, tea and social evening) will be field at Liverpool. Mr. H. B. Eldon (president of the Man- chester group) was elected president of the new federation ano Mr. L. J. Weat, (the secretary of the St. Helen's group and who had in hand the organiza- tion of the conference) was elected secretary. After the conference the Esperantists sat down to tea and subsequently a concert was held. Songs were sung, recitations given and speeches delivered entirely in Esperanto. . PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. "The Lady of Haigh." With an attraction of such peculidr local interest as "The Lady of Haigh "it is to be regretted that the audiences at the Parr Hall have not been larger, and we would advise all who have not seen this powerful play to take the oppoitunity of doing so to-night. Founded upon incidents in the history of the ancestors lof the present- Lord Balcarres, of Haigh Hall, near PSI Meetings. i Wigan, the play, it is claimed,' gives the story of The Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) presided at the meet- , Mao's Cross, a well-known' landmark in Smndishgate, jug of the Wycliffe Society, on Sunday. Mr. Taylb Wigan. with an eventful period in the lives of ther of i "in which Bradshaighs, of Haigh, as its ground work, the Tither character eand creed I hHeoll made od allguasvioen thet o address conduet o 7 , has succeeded in weaving an absorbing narrative, and Mr Barnes sang feelingly " Behold Me standing at the there is DO lack of excitement. The last Act in which door," 'lie the villainous Sir Osmond Neville is slain by Sir also 1 wheilatr ttibloyuj osianvien theg in the ocphloerurs; mr.. William Bradshaigh at Newton Park is exceptionally s a theng ‘ brethrenlvhen P