        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A pale and .clit,hevelled Frenchman was sink- ing into his deck-chair on a Channel steamer when a passenger asked, cheerily :—"Ah, mon- sieur, have you breakfasted?" "No, mon- sieur," answered the pallid Frenchman, "I haf not breakfasted—on ze contrairy."</p>
        <p>There was a young girl in the choir, Whose voice rose hoir and hoir, Till it reached such a height It was clear out of sight, And they found it next day in the spoir.</p>
        <p>When a girl falls in love it changes her wbole nature. She will even act pleased the first time her escort steps on her dress and rips it out of shape. "I suppose," said the farm hand who was look- ing for a job, "you believe in the eight-hour system?" "Yes,'l answered the farmer. "I work eight hours in the forenoon, and eight in the afternoon, except in harvest times, then I usually put in three or four hours extra." "Sir," utters the haughty maiden, when the impulsive youth slips his arm about her taper waist;. "sir, what is the meaning of this? HaVf a care, sir Do not go too far!" "Too far?" he whispers, contractin4 his biceps muscle; "toe far? How long an arm do you think I have? It won't go any farther!"</p>
        <p>Bystander: "Should you say that picture was taken from life?" Critic: "f dolt% know; but the world wouldn't suffer if the artist was."</p>
        <p>Mr. Sickly: "Now, doctor, tell me, candidly, in what condition do you find my lungs?" Dr. Soo-rover: "They are in bad shape, but there ii no danger of their not lasting as long as you live."</p>
        <p>"Cyrus Winterside," snapped the indignant wife as the quarrel waxed fiercer, "you married me for my money, and you know it!""TiF false !" roared Mr. Winterside. "I married you for your amiable disposition, you virago !"</p>
        <p>A crusty old fellow once asked: "What is the reason that griffins and d.iagous are ladies' favou- rite subjects for embroidery designs?" "Ah, because they are continually thinking of their husbands," was the lady's quick retort. Leap year is having its effect. A young man called on a girl a few evenings ago and she com- plimented him on his new suit of clothes, and styled it his wedding suit. "But this is a busi- ness suit." he said. "Well," replied the young lady, firmly, "I mean business !"</p>
        <p>Husband: "How much clid you.ipend to-day?" Wife: "g,2 18s. 6!, :I." Husband (ironically) : "Was that all?" Wife (with an injured sir): "That was all I had." , ,</p>
        <p>"W,iat made young Lightpaye leave lectors,. the: guests sat down to supper?" "The host. who can't see very well without his glasses, told him there was a smudge on his lip." "And there?" "Nothing but his moustache!" It is a good thing to enjoy life when you are single. After you are married you may not get a,chance.</p>
        <p>Wifey : "0 George! I do believe the little darling is going to have another tooth." Hubby: "Great Scott! Can't we send him to a hospital till it's over?"</p>
        <p>Nobody outside the journalistic profession has any idea how difficult it is for an editor to please some of his potions. For instance, referfing to a public man's reputation for carelessness in the matter of his toilet, a paper announced. "Mr. Maguire will wash himself before he assumes the °Mee of town councillor." This made Maguire rurious, and he demanded a retraction, which ap- peared thus :—"Mr. Maguire requests us to deny that he will wash himself before he assumes the office of town councillor." Oddly enough ; this only enraged Maguire the More. "Let's go out and see what's burnin'," said Pat to Mike, at the theatre. "What d'ye mean?" "Those two men behind us said they were goin' to the foyer."</p>
        <p>'Tis love that makes the old world go, As oft bath been repeated; 'Tis love that sets the sun aglow To keep our planet heated; 'Tis love that makes th_e, man sublime And makes the lady winning, And money many, many a time Is that same love's beginning.</p>
        <p>A dentist was saved from drowning by a labourer, and from the depths of his grateful heart exclaimed:--"Noble, brave, gallant man, how shall I reward you? Only Come to my house, ! and I will cheerfully pull out every tooth you have in your head, and not charg'e you a six- pence !"</p>
        <p>• Mr. Squills (pompously): "I've had a great • runny grave eases in the past two'months." Mr. t G,ipea (a rival) : "So ,Grimes, the undertaker, 'tells me."</p>
        <p>'Cilia : "Just as the train was leaving, the most handsome man e7deied the carriage, and sat down beside me. Unt ortunately, however, he got 'out at the first s!nt,on." Flo: "Of course ; tie olldn't, get out before."</p>
        <p>"Violet, dearest, dg you play that tune often?" asked Hugh Montre'ssor of his affianced. "Yes pet, and when we are married I'll play it all the i time." Then II ugh went out and shuddered him- self to death. •</p>
        <p>"I cannot _understand, sir, why you permit your daughter to sue me for breach of promise You remember that you were bitterly opposed to our engagement, because I wasn't good enough for her, and would disgrace the family." "Young man, that was sentiment ; this is business.','</p>
        <p>" Hallo, youngster ! What are you doing in the City?" "Oh, I'm on the Stock Exchange aow:" "What, a boy like you? What are you going to do there?" "Try, to earn an honest living.' "Really! Well, you ought to succeed; there's no competition !" - -</p>
        <p>If,a woman wants a welcome when she returns fume, she should leave her husband alone with Hie baby when she goes. Mabel: "Why didn't you scream when he put his arms around you?" Ethel: "I wanted to, but couldn't, `and when I could I didn't want to." Lady Friend: "I understand your husband is of a retiring disposition." Wife : "Ye—es, but usually not before 3 a.m."</p>
        <p>Superstition has never yet kept anyone from accepting thirteen for a dozen.</p>
