      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS. _- ANNUAL DINNER AT WARRINGTON. -- A Critic of Garden Suburbs.</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS. _- ANNUAL DINNER AT WARRINGTON. -- A Critic of Garden Suburbs.</p>
        <p>By L. G. Gniozza Monay, M.P.</p>
        <p>My DEAR CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>I have been very pleased indeed with the number of letters I have received containing suggestions for a new Competition. Further down these columns you twill see a letter printed received from Gladys Graver. I think this a very good idea, arid have pleasure in announcing it as our first Competition, which will be open for the next four weeks. At the present time it is really extraordinary the value one can get for the sum of a penny. I wonder how many of you have passed down Ludgate Hill in London during Christmas week. I have seen the hawkers—men, women, and children— standing shoulder to shoulder down its .entire length selling for the most part penny toys. Wonderful productions I have seen of toys and books, even. penny Testaments. How such a variety of articles can be produced and sold at this rate I cannot understand. Think of the pleasure that some little toys would give to poor children who cannot even afford a penny. To the senders of the six best penny toys I shall give presents. If any of my members wish to ask me any questions write me at once, and I will try to reply in next week's paper. With love, UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—Thank you so much for the beautiful box of Fry's chocolates, which I re- ceived safely this morning. I never thought I should be so lucky as to win a prize, and was agreeably surprised when I saw my name last week among the winners. lam glad there were so many entries of clothes for the Competition. Many poor people would be grate- ful for them this Christmas, I am sure. Don't you think a Toy Competition would be very popular? Each competitor could try and get the best toy for the sum of one penny—as you announced in the "Corner "—and they must not exceed that .limit. Of course, the toys received could all be sent to some children's hospital; so it would be exciting for those who entered and the entries would amuse the poor ehildrdn in the hospital. Again thanking you for the chocolates,—l remain, your "Niece of the Corner," GLADYS GRAVER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WELCOMR TO NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>WELCOMR TO NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Lily Jones, A. Charlesworth, Lily Short, and Harry Short. Mirrors have been sent to Rebecca Ashworth, Florrie Mercer. (I shall be pleased to send little pocket-mirrors to all sen.ders of contri- butions. Kindly state in your letter if a mirror is required.)</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l have not received any- thing for the story nor the names fiat I sent. I eent 100 and odd names. My brother James Henry sent you a story and 80 names, but he has not received anything.—From your nephew, ERNEST DONEY. SOMETHING BLIND.</p>
        <p>In one of the crowded streets of New York a beggar was in the habit of taking his stand every day, and appealing to the charity of the passers by. By means of a short string he held a dog, round thq neck of which a card was fastened, with rifle words "I am blind." A very kind old man, who had been in the habit of dropping a penny into the beggar's hat, passed rapidly one morning without doing so. Instantly the beggar rushed after him, and asked for the penny to be given as usual. The gentleman, turning in surprise, said, "Why, I thought you were blind?" "Oh, no, sir," was the cool reply, "it is the dog that is blind, as the card says. FLORRIE MERCER.</p>
        <p>A BUTTERFLY. A butterfly basked on a baby's grave, Where a lily had chanced to grow: "Why art thou here, with the gaudy dye, When she of the blue and sparkling eye Must sleep in the churchyard low?" Then it lightly soared through the sunny air, And spoke from its shining track: "I was a worm till I won my wings, And she whom thou mournest like a seraph sings,— Wouldst thou call the blest one back?" Jussin GOULBOTTRNE.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l now take the pleasure in writing you a few lines to thank you for the beautiful box of chocolates which I received from you. I was delighted when I took the parcel from the postman and saw it was from Uncle Ned, and I think it is a most beautiful present, and I value it very much. I showed it to my friends, and they said it was a beautiful box.—l remain, your loving niece,</p>
        <p>FLORENCE KIVELL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MIGRATION OF SWIFTS.</head>
        <p>THE MIGRATION OF SWIFTS.</p>
        <p>Nobody has yet been able to solve satis- factorily the mystery of the swifts' migration. The birds do not arrive in these island § until May, and yet they are for the most part ready to go by the middle of August. The first north- west wind after that date generally decides the question for the majority, but there are always few laggards, who remain until the end of the month. A swift is a rare sight in September. Swallow and martin are with us a much longer time.</p>
        <p>It is recorded by good authority that a French Man learning English and anxious to say something very striking in parting from the lady of hie heart, carefully consulted his dictionary, and there finding that "to pickle" meant "to preserve," bade her farewell with the emphatic exclamation, "May heaven pickle you I " 0</p>
        <p>A celebrated French preacher, in a sermon on t'he duties of wives, said: "I see in this con- gregation a woman who has been guilty of disobedience to her husband, and in order to point her obt I will fling my breviary at her head." He lifted his book, and every female head instantly ducked.</p>
