      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BROKEN TULIP.</head>
        <p>THE BROKEN TULIP.</p>
        <p>Henry was in the garden one day playing with his hoop, and it so happened that the hoop rolled on a flower-bed and broke a fine tulip, which his father prized very much. Just then his mother came from the house. "Look," said she, "the high wind has broken this beautifuJ tulip!" "It was not the wind that broke it, mother," said Henry, "it was I who broke it." "You, Henry! How could you do it?" "I was playing with my hoop and it ran against the flower." "But you have been told not to play in this part of the garden, Henry." "Yes, mother, and I am very sorry that I came here." "And so am I. Your father will be much grieved at the loss of this flower, which he prized so highly." Henry's mother then went in to breakfast, and as he was about to follow her, John, the gardener's son, came up to him and said, "Why, Henry, if you had held your tongue your father would have thought the same as your mother did: that the wind' had broken the tulip. I should never have told anything about it, you may be sure." "I never tell a falsehood," said Henry. "It would not have been telling one," replied John. "It would have been just the same," said Henry, "I should have been trying to deceive my mother and father." "Right, my boy, right!" said Henry's father, who had overheard, the boys talking. He 1;acl come to rebuke Henry for breaking the flower, but now he said he would rather lose all the flowers in his garden than think that his son would try to deceive him. Then, turning to John, he said: "As for you, John, take this lesson to heart; and re- member that it is not the mere form of words but the intention to deceive that makes the lie." L. HOLT. P.S.—ln winter lots of little birds come in our garden, and I always put crumbs out for them. One day there were a lot of sparrows out and one was lame; we could not catch it or else we should have looked after it.—L.H.</p>
