      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MIXING THE LESSONS.</head>
        <p>MIXING THE LESSONS.</p>
        <p>A school inspector, who had an important appointment to keep later in the day, was examining a school in a country district some distance from a railway station. He was afraid of losing his train; so, hurrying with his work, he tried to do two things at once. Standing in the doorway between two rooms, he gave dictation to Standard 11. in the main room, and at the same time dictated a sum to Standard 111. in the class-room, jerking out a few words alternately. The sum was: "If a couple of tat ducks cost as., how many. could be got for 4272 10s. 9d?" The dictation for Standard IL began: "Now, as a lion, prowling about in search," etc. Of course, the children heard both and got a bit mixed. One youngster's dictation commenced: "Now, a couple of fat ducks, prowling about in search of a lion, who had lost 95."; while a Standard 111. lad was puzzling his brains over the following sum: "If seventy-two couples of fat lions cost 95., how much prowling about I could be got for £72 10s. 9d. ? " _</p>
        <p>MAUD MOBLEY.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l read the paper every week, and I am very interested in the "Children's Corner," and I wish to become a member of your League. I am sending you a story, called</p>
        <p>THE PHILOSOPHER AND THE CAT.</p>
        <p>I once saw a lecturer upon experimental philosophy place a cat under the glass receiver of an air pump for the purpose of demonstrat- ing that life cannot be supported without air and respiration. The lecturer had already made several strokes with the piston, in order to exhaust the receiver of its air, when the cat, beginning to feel herself very uncomfortable in the rarified atmosphere, was fortunate enough to discover the source from whence her uneasi- ness proceeded. She placed her paw upon the hole through which the air escaped, and thus prevented any more escaping from the re- ceiver. All the exertions of the philosopher were now unavailing; in vain he drew the piston, and the cat's paw effectually prevented its operation. Hophig to effect his purpose, he again let the air into the receiver, which, as soon as the cat perceived, she withdrew her paw from the aperture, but whenever he attempted to exhaust the receiver she applied her paw as before. The spectators clapped their hands in admiration of the cat's sagacity, and the lecturer was compelled to remove her and substitute another cat who possessed less penetration for the cruel experiment. SUSAN HUGHES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SOMETIME-SOMEWHERE.</head>
        <p>SOMETIME-SOMEWHERE.</p>
        <p>You gave on the way a pleasant smile, And thought no more about it; But it bheered a life that was IS ad the while</p>
        <p>That Might have been wrecked without it— And for this smile, with its fruitage fair, You'll reap a crown, sometime, somewhere, You spoke one day a cheering word, And passed to other duties; But it warmed a heart, and promise stirred, , And painted a life with beauties— - And for the word and its silent prayer ' You'll reap a palm, sometime, somewhere. You lent a hand to a fallen one, _</p>
        <p>A lift in kindness given; But it ea.ved a soul when hope was none, And won a heart for heaven-- And for the help you proffered there You'll reap a joy, sometime, somewhere.</p>
        <p>The most tender-hearted man we ever heard of was a shoemaker, who always shut his eyes and whistled when he ran his awl into a sole.</p>
        <p>Germantown has a -young lady so proficient in piano playing that upon imitating a storm the other night over a hundred people caught cold from the dampness that prevailed.</p>
        <p>The following is a remarkably neat instance of the retort direct: A husband advertised that he, Thomas A—, would no longer be answer- able for the debts incurred by his wife. There- 'won the wife replied: 'This is to notify that I, Elizabeth A—, am able to pay all my own debts now -that I have got shot of Tommy."</p>
        <p>F 0 0 IBA L LS i. , . a Given away weekly. w Every competitor receives something.</p>
        <p>EVERY TUESDAY during DECEMBER, JANUARY and FEBRUARY we will rive away SIX SPLENDID FOOTBALLS for the six most interesting Postcards we receive during the preceding Iseek. YOU CAN PUT ON THE BACK or YOUR POSTCARD either A GOOD JOKE, STORYETPE, CONUNDRUM, PUZZLE, SKETCH, or anything else that you think is iliteresting—ANYTHlNG BUT A LIMERICK—and if your postcard is among the SIX best received in the week in which it is sent, you will receives FOOTBALL. To every competitor, be he or she PRIZE WINNER or not, will be sent a sample bottle of</p>
        <p>MASON'S COFFEE ESSENCE.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS YOUR POSTCARD TO-DAY: &amp; Mason, Nottingham.</p>
        <p>In 1/- &amp; 2/6 Tins; also 4d. Packets.</p>
