      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Presentation at St. Clement's.</head>
        <p>Presentation at St. Clement's.</p>
        <p>In the echo 4room of the St. Clement's Mission, Bank-street, on Sunday afternoon, Mr. Taylor, Manchester-road, was presented with a handsome oak framed group phov graph of himself and the members of the Youno. Men's Bible Class by the members, as a token of their gratitude to him for, his valuable services as teacher of the class since its organisation until about three years ago when the post was passed over by him to Mr. Steel, the present teacher. Mr. Taylor said he thanked them very much for their present which was indeed a handsome one and he was pleased to see the good work he had done for the mission. He uras glad to say it had always been a source of pleasure to him to teach the gospel to such a class of young men as those who were present during the afternoon. It was also gratifying to see so many new faces in the class and he hoped that there would be many more to come.— (Applause.) —A vote of thanks was passed to the Rev. T. W. Barber for being present, and also to Mr. Taylor for his kindness in distributing the prizes for the past year's good attendance to the young men. It is worthy of note that Mr. Taylor has had about 40 years' experience in teaching the Gospel, most of which time was spent in Oldham, once occupying the position of President of the Oldham Sunday Schools Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>Because you can breathe the rich medicine from Peps straight into your lungs and bronchi 4 , tubes, Peps are the one home - medicire you need for coughs, colds and ; bronchitis. r,/i tS-'219. ; • , "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>AIR RESISTANCE TO TRAINS.</p>
        <p>In a paper on the predetermination of train-resistance, read by Mr. C. A. Carus- Wilson before the Institution of Civil En- gineers, one of the practical conclusions arrived at was that the resistance of the sir with a train of bogie-coaches, running at sixty miles per hour, amounts to about one- half the total tractive effort required to haul the train. Experiments conducted by the St. Louis Electnc Railway Test Commission shew that a large reduction, can be wade in the front and rear air-resistance by shaping the ends, and that by this means a saring can be effected of 10 per cent. of the total tractive effort with a loons passenger train, and 30 per cent. with a single coach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NITROGEN-FIXING FUNGI.</head>
        <p>NITROGEN-FIXING FUNGI.</p>
        <p>The higher plants depend largely for the needful nitrogen on species of fungi which are able to assimilate this element from the atmosphere. A series of investigations by Charlotte Ternetz, of which an account is given in the Jahrbuch of botanical science, indicate that this is the case to a much larger extent than hitherto supposed. Species of heath which naturally grow in soils poor in nitrogenous matter frequently have their roots covered with a fungous growth which supplies them with nitrogen. The power of absorbing nitrogen varies in different species of fungi. And it is a curious fact that some of them only fix the atmo- spheric nitrogen when grown in a culture free from this element. If there is nitro- genous matter in the culture, they seem to prefer to avail 'themselves of this instead of exerting their powers of fixation.</p>
        <p>GAS BURNER WHICH CUTS METALS.</p>
        <p>I The existence of a' means of opening within a few minutes the largest fireproof safe by a comparatively simple apparatus is I not generally known, though that it can be done has been demonstrated by two burglars. The method is, according to the Engineer- ing Review, that of cutting the metal by a burner utilising oxygen-acetylene or oxygen and ordinary illuminating gee. The experiment of burning iroli wire in pure oxygen gas is a well-known one, and the step of applying this phenomenon indus- trially was made some time ago in the weld- ing of metals autogenously with the oxygen flame. In the application of this method for cutting metals a burner is employed, b./ whieh the oxygen supply is directed to the melting line only. . . . The plate to be cut is preheatAcl along a line to be cut with a normal welding flame of acetylene-oxygen, illuminating gas oxygen, or benzine-oxygen --it does not matter which and then a strong oxygen current supplied through an additional small tube is directed along this line. The least expensive is the acetylene- oxygen flame, especially when thick plates are to be cut, since this gee mixture evolves the greatest number of calories per unit of gai. volume. In practice, however, illuminat- ing gas or benzine may be used instead of gas or acetylene for the sole of convenienoe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>LUMINOUS FROGS AND CRABS.</head>
        <p>LUMINOUS FROGS AND CRABS.</p>
        <p>The flesh of most sea fishes and other marine animals becomes more or less luminous within a day or two After death. The light is emitted, however, not by the flesh itself, but by certain bacteria which can be collected from its surface, and which are of common occurrence in ma water. A similar appearance, due also to the presence of luminous bacteria, is often I presented by meet. Those bacteria are harmless to human beings, as they cannot live at a temperature , above 76deg. Fahr., and the temperature of the human body is %deg. Fehr., but living cold-blooded animele can be inoculated with luminous bacteria, with surprising moults. The Russian phyeiologist Tarchanoff inocu- lated frogs with luminous bacteria obtained from the Baltic. The baaWria multiplied in the blood, and caused the entire body of the frog to emit light. The luminescence, which was especially intense in the tongue and other soft parts, continued three or four days. Similar phenomena have been ob- served to occur naturally in cold-blooded animals. A few years ago the French natu- ralist Giard found among the sand hoppers that Swarmed On the beach at Wimereux one which, instead of hopping, crawled slowly over the sand and glowed brightly. On examination, the body oA the little crustacean was found to be filled with luminous bacteria. When other sand hoppers were inoculated with its blood, they also be- came luminous, gradually lost , strength, and died, but continued to glow for several hours after death. . THE POISON-BOWL IN ANIMAL LIFE.</p>
        <p>It is a matter of common knowledge that a very considerable number of animals pre- serve themselves from enemies, and also paralyse their prey through the poeiteasion and use of poison-apparatus. Typically, of course, the snakes that is to say, the venomous species—will at once occur to the mind as representative of the poison-bowl and dag.g,cr in animal life; but there are many other illustrations to be found in the zoological scale of analogous means of offence or defence, as the case may be, writes Dr. Andrew Wilson in the Bluth-rated London News. From the jellyfishes and their neighbours upwards, poison glands and darts Inv by no means infrequently met with. The insects are thus arrned, a few of them, and among fishes and lizards venomous instinct are now and thee apparent. The means whereby poison is used in lower life as an offensive or defensive agent vary; that is to say, the apparatus itself is not uniform in its nature and structure. True, there is a gene- ral plan to be discerned underlying the actual variations presented by venomous ex- pedients. There is, first of all, the poison itself, manufactured or secreted, as the physiologist would say, in a gland or similar receptacle; and, second, there is the means whereby the venom can be introduced into the body of opponent or prey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>How THE POISON 18 SECRETED.</head>
        <p>How THE POISON 18 SECRETED.</p>
        <p>The poison is itself notable becaisee of its representing an animal accretion, which, like the harmless fluids and the necessary ones in animals such as the digestive jukes is elaborated from the blood. Here we are face to face with the great work of the living cell as the essential unit of the animal frame. Just as certain cells manufacture saliva, and as others secrete bile, and others, again, gastric juice or tears, so living unite are set apart in the animal body for the pur- pose of elaborating poison. The difference between a harmless animal secretion and a venomous one does not, therefore, consist in the raw material, the blood, from which it is formed, but in the constructive power of the living cells which are set apart for its secretion. It is this clietinct individuality of the microscopic units of the animal body which constitutes one of the most striking phases of life's work and action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>How THE POISON IS DISCHARGED.</head>
        <p>How THE POISON IS DISCHARGED.</p>
        <p>The simplest poison-apparatus is possibly that seen in the jellyfishes. All these organ- isms are provided with stinging-cells, called "thread - cells"; but in many species the darts have no power of penetrating the human epidermis. The common species of jellyfishes can be handled freely without un- pleasant results, but malty kinds, on the other hand, sting very severely indeed. Each " thread-cell" is really a minute capsule dis- tended to the full with an irritating fluid, the poison. Coiled up within the cell is a thread-like filament. Whenever the cell is touched it bursts open, the thread is erected, end the poison emitted. The thread we may regard as a kind of. dart capable of intro- ducing the poison into the tissues of the attacked being. The discharge of hundreds of those minute bombs ninv very well account for the irritation the jellyfish stings produce. In the case of the poisonous snake, two modified teeth represent the "fangs." Each tooth is hollow, and the canals of the teeth are continuations of that which leads from each Poison-gland. Comnression of the gland by a special muscle forces the poison down the canal and into the wounds which the teeth hare made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
