      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Warrington Avenged on Wigan.</head>
        <p>Warrington Avenged on Wigan.</p>
        <p>The Wigan men paid their return visit to Warrington on Saturday and brought a strong side only to find the locals in a very aggressive mood. Warring- ton had several of their best men still absent through 'injury and sickness but they quite overplayed the visitors and handsomely avenged the defeat of the previous week. After a very wet and stormy morning, the afternoon was fine, though a gale of wind blew down the ground towards the railway goal. Wigan brought a lot of supporters, and there was a fine crowd of about 7,000. _Teams and officials</p>
        <p>Warrington—Tilley, back • Fish, Jordan, Dickenson, and Watkin, three-quarter backs; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs ; Shugars, Thomas, Naylor, Jenkins, Belton, and Harmer, forwards. Wigan—Sharrock, back; Leytham, Jenkins, Todd and Trebarne, three-quarter backs ; David and J. Thomas, half-backs; Ramsdale, Cheatham, Sileock, Wiloock Brooks and Burton forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. F. Renton, Hunslet. Touch judges Messrs. J. Edwards, St. Helens, and S. Hassall,Salford. The home team had the advantage of a gale during the first half, and after Fish had placed his side in an advantageous position a brilliant round of passing ended in Watkin scoring at the corner in the first live minutes. Sharrock ran strongly to the Warrington quarters, and in reply Fish dribbled rapidly along touch and forcing the ball past Sharrock, Shugars raced up to score at the corner. The Warringtonians still continued to attack strongly, and after Watkin had taken the ball over and lost possession, Brooks completely outwitted David and Jenkins, and scored near the corner, this time Thomas kicking a fine goal. Wigan had a chance when Todd broke away, but Fish saved his lines splendidly, and at half-time Warrington led by a goal and three tries (11 points) to nil. Turning round Warrington again forced matters, and after Dickenson had effected a fine run down the centre ' sharp transfers between Naylor and Brooks led up to Fish scoringeplendidly. Wigan attackel, but lacked finish, and repeatedly Fish placed the locals in strong positions, on one occasion racing from his quarter-flag and being hauled down a yard from the posts. Fine work by the home forwards quite neutralised the advan- tage Wigan had from the wind. Near the close Wigan madea smart rally, and Jenkins scored. Result : Warrington 1 goal and 4 tries (14 points), Wigan 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Friendly Match.</head>
        <p>Friendly Match.</p>
        <p>WIGAN " A " V. WARRINGTON "A."-3,000 spectators assembled at Central Park, Wigan, for this match on Saturday. Warrington were very weakly represented. Wigan started in fine style. Battersby, receiving a wide pass from Jones, quickly got away and when within a few yards of the Warrington line he handed to Price, who crossed, Coulthard just failing at goal. Daintith made a brilliant run for Warrington, and he was instru- mental in placing Wigan on the defensive. Tinckler scored at the corner. The Wigan men had all the play after this and ran up the big score of 3 goals, 9 tries to Warrington 1 try. Result: Wigan " A," three goals nine tries (33 points) ; Warrington " A " one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
