evil results which the theft of the gold brings what I am entitled to say. Let me clear up as Igo about, has probably never been so well sung. The along. I am accused of ridiculing the use of the spontaneous cheer which went up from every part of terms "stored toil " and " unconsumed labour " as the crowded house when the curtain fell at the close of applied to capital. I did so because capital is the the first act clearly expressed the gratification and 'stored savings of consumed labour." I may borrow possibly the sense of pride felt by the audience. The capital and gamble with it on the Stock Exchange, or six recalls accorded the singers was a well-deserved race course, or spend it on Limerick coupons, but I compliment." could not do this with" stored toil " or' unconsumed THE STANDARD. labour" which remain for ever the sole monopoly of ~ From the moment of his entrance, spent and weary the worker and indeed, constitute in tbe majority of when he falls prostrate on the hearth of his enemy, Mr. cases his only capital. lam also accused of refusing to Walter Hyde, the Siegmund, commanded the attention answer his argument. If declining for the moment to of the audience, and held them by an impersonation of answer a question illogit:tilly. applied constitutes my -,, artistic sren.tth which was as surprising as it was corn- _ ~ .. .</p>
        <p>ment will not again ~low weir ...Jae au, _ wasted but will exhaust their constitutional powers in order to force the matter to an issue so that the harm done both to religion and education since the unsettle- ment in 1902 may be stayed. Rev. C. Harvey-Cook. Another lucid effort to solve a vexed question. Will it be abortive ? One respects Hr. McKenna's effort— whether his Bill is momentous or not is beside the mark—but it is concise, cogent, clean-cut. The Non- conformist parent has two clear reliefs, namely—that State schools shall be under public control and the teachers in them free from denominational tests. Mr. Balfour's Act of 1902 co-ordinated education,yet seemed to lean too much Churchwards. Mr. McKenna's Bill segregates the schools but seems to lean too much Chapelwards. It is a pity that Mr. Birrell's Bill did not have a chance. One is sorry not to be able to greet this last effort with a cheer. Where Mr. Birrell's Bill broke down in 1900, will this of Mr. McKenna's survive? The object of this Bill is to take the management of the schools out of the hands of denominational control and place it in the hands of the public. This is surely sound legislation. That it may entail very considerable sacrifice of place and power on the part of many clerics goes without question. I am a Nonconformist, but I am also an Englishman, and my love of fair play arouses my sympathies for the denominational managers of whom we are asking larger chivalry ; namely, the sacrifice of their long-established prerogative as school managers. Is it too much to hope that they will be nobly altrusistic ? that the extremists on both sides will become reasonable? What we as educationalists need stand for, is not what will con- ciliate the Church, or what will conciliate the Chapel. What we have to find is the greatest common measure in the matter of national education ; as it is we seem bent on finding the least common measure. One enter- tains grave fears in respect to the contracting clause. True the Bill edges the clause round with stiffening conditions as to Government inspections and examina- tions, but, unless it is a gift by one hand to be taken back by the other, such contracting out appears to have in it the seeds of educational inelficiency.Of his monetary regulations, which will hit the voluntary schools very severely, one can only urge that the denominationalists being sincere in their plea for the right to instruct their school children in the tenets of their faith, will not find 13s. per child per annum an exorbitant exaction for the right, especially in view of the 475. per child per annum, which they are to have if the Bill becomes law. Perhaps if we could agree to have the Bible taught as literature we might find our controversies at an end. That this Bill will provoke hostility is certain. It is too parochial, not sufficiently universal, yet if thrown out many lead to cutting the kr ' ,nd establishi hat is called, though I</p>
        <p>on so difficult and discordant a subject as that of religions or Biblical teaching ! I suppose the Roman Catholics did not accept that syllabus, but they had then' as they would have still, their remedy. My earnest desire is that this great National Question should find a wise and peaceful permanent settlement. But, really, so long as men of light and leading" approach it in the spirit of rancorons partizanship, as shown in the speech of the Leader of the Opposition in the House of Commons, and in the published utterances of extreme Anglicans and irreconcileable Romans, I confess to being very doubtful of any such desirable result. Rather, my belief is that eventually the nation will be driven to the Secular Solution. Much as, from motives largely sentimental, I should deprecate that result, I do not believe in its ultimate religious disadvantage. Churches and parents, between them, would then have to accept the responsibility in the matter of religion which really is their own, and have to care for the religious training of childhood and youth. And I will add this word, that if that settlement of the religious difficulty in National Elementary Education shall be reached, none will have done more to render it inevitable than those, both clerical and lay, who now are using violent language, and creating widespread bitterness, in the sacred name of religion ! Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P. The new Education Bill introduced by Mr. McKenna in the House of Commons on Monday night Will probably meet the same fate as Mr. Birrell's Bill. It is an honest attempt to satisfy the demand for Denomin- ational Education. The almost savage onslaught made by Mr. Balfour on the Bill immediately on its intro- duction indicates a determination to make it impossible for the present Government to settle this vexed ques- tion. The fact that it is a Bill coming from a Liberal Government is sufficient to arouse Mr. Balfour's fierce opposition. Then unfortunately he knows too well that he can do pretty much as he likes with the House of Lords and he can play his little game at his own sweet will. But the country is getting sick and tired of this vexed controversy and he may find, as he has found before, that the ratepayer and voter will have none of his vexatious meddling. I have no hesita- tion in saying that the vast majority of the people do not want secular and sectarian education mixed together in a confused medley. They are quite willing to pay for Secular Education and Bible Teaching; but they are resolved that the rates shall not be used for sectarian teaching. Many indeed object to the taxes of the country being used in any degree for this purpose. The fact of the matter is that the demand for sectarian education comes largely from the sacerdotal party which is not trusted whether it comes from the Roman Catholic or Anglican Catholic Church. It is a priestly party with which the vast majority of of this countr have no s- ,ath, Small</p>
        <p>tams— -gi ty seem fair and reasonable. To the majority of Anglicans and of Roman Catholics, however, such transfers and the process of extinction of which they are a part appear not merely undesirable, but as little better than criminal, since they involve breaches of the conditions under which large sums of money have been contributed and violate the conscientious con- victions of large numbers of Christian parents. No one in touch with the policy of the Board of Education will anticipate any appreciable relief to the rates from this bill. Those who really care for educa- tional progress would prefer Parliament to leave the subject alone for a few years : the full effects of the Act of 1902, which like that of 1870, marks an era in the history of Education in England, have not yet been felt ; and too frequent and drastic changes seriously interfere with healthy development in social as well as in natural history. Rev. E C. E. Carleton. The new Education Bill quite confirms the appre- hensions that Churchmen were led to entertain as to its character. From a brief and necessarily cursory glanoe at its provisions the following seem to be its main features :- 1. Broadly speaking it is an attempt to force Non- conformists' principles of religious teaching on all parents whether they like it or not—bribery being the means for bringing the force to bear, except in cases where persecution may be more easily resorted to. 2. The Nonconformist scheme of religious instruc- tion is to he formulated by the London County Coun- cil, the Free Churchmen being quite willing to receive it at their hands—this at least indicates the character of the Free Church religion. 3. Parents who prefer to have any other kind of religious teaching can only have what they want at the expense of a teaching, pecuniary penalty. 4. This favour shown to the Free Churchmen is entirely on Nonconformist grounds. It cannet be on Educational grounds, as they have in the past done less for Education than any other section of the community. 5. The method of dealing with trusts in the Bill is at once immoral and irreligious. Trusts have always been regarded as of sacred as well as of legal obliga- tion, from which obligation no trustee can be dis— charged except by a Court of Equity. The Government in this Bill are adopting a method for bringing pecuniary pressure to beat on unwilling trustees to compel them to break their trustee obligations. 5. The cry of "no tests " for teachers is at once ignorant and mischievous. It means that someone is to be told off to give religions teaching whose com- petency to give it is not to be enquired into. As long as religious teaching is given, the competency of those who give it must be ascertained, just as the competency of the secular teacher has to be enquired into with</p>
        <p>had sent back something like 8,000 and before the end of the year they would have sent back 18,000. They hoped before the Government went out of power that they would have cleared the whole of the Transvaal of Chinamen. (Cheers.) Councillor NIELD (Opposition Leader) : Arising out of that question I would ask the Secretary of the Colonies whether when he has dispatched the Chinamen home, he proposes to substitute coolies from Mada- gascar to take their place? Mr. FUFLNESS asked for notice of the question to be given.</p>
        <p>. - - .. .. ~. offence, then I plead guilty, but even the proverbial lete. His v poice was at once sympathetic and telling, blind man ought to see that I gave my opinion pretty and it is not too much to say that such even singing in plainly in the moral to my little parody at the close this part has not come from any Germn tenor whom of my last letter, to the effect that neither the borrower we have had at Covent Garden. Mr. Hyde, moreover, nor the lender are justified, both transactions being acted with point, and, with all his careful and delicate equally detrimental to the common weal, attention to vocal phrasing, he infused a rugged vitality I agree with Mr. Parker that it has become essential and energy into his reading that made it essentially we should compare definitions as to what we mean by distinctive." Interest. I note also his suggestion, that we keep as ;NIORNING LEADER. near as possible to the definitions of the old school, " Most people agreed that the music of Siegmund had which hardly coincides with his willingness to adopt never anywhere been sung with such charm as by Mr. mine. Evidently Mr. Parker suspects I dare hold Walter Hyde, who at one bound has stepped into the opinions of my own. Certain it is I obtain my economics front rank of Wagnerian tenors. This may sound a first band, neither going to the man in the street, nor wild exaggeration, but I am sure that very soon the to the theorists. As a definition Mr. Parker's " payment whole musical world will agree. His singing of the for borrowed capital " may he broad enough, for Spring Song was exquisite,and one could point to many . . .. . . . . . . . . . . ....</p>
        <p>the people Jr this country sympathy. as this party is, it is obtrusive and loud in its demands. To meet this demand the Government offers in the Bill to allow them, if the parents of the cbildren wish it, to contract out, and to have their schools to themselves to manage as they wish on the understanding that the secular education of the children does not suffer. They may go with the assurance that they shall receive 475. yearly for every child from the taxation of the country. They are also to he allowed to charge fees not to exceed 9d. a week. Possibly 13s. per child will be required in fees and voluntary contributions. Surely not a very large amount to raise if there is really a great demand for sectarian teaching ! If the wealthy laity of the Anglican Church will not help to this extent they are not very keen for it. There is little ,bt UT— the Ron- - Catholics ill be able to</p>
        <p>regard to the Objects he is called upon to teach. Po proceed on the principle that anybody can give Biblical instruction whether competent or not is simply an attempt to degrade religion. Rev. F. Vaughan. Not having yet seen the text of the Bill, it is some- what difficult to give an opinion. but so far as I have bean able to gather the salient features of the measure, it is in my judgment calculated to provide a reasonable and workable basis for the establishment of a really national system of Education under popular control. It will sem:re simple Bible teaching in all provided schools, without which any English system of educa- tion would be hopelessly incomplete. It will be a charter of liberty, of untold value, in villages and</p>
        <p>Gordian knot and esta— !ng what is ... cannot understand why, secular education. Canon Stevenson. Speaking at the annual conversazione in connection with the Warrington Church Society, which was held in St. Paul's schools last evening, Canon Morley Stevenson alluded to the Education Bill. " Whether the Bill passes or not " he said, "it is quite impossible that it could prove a settlement of the question. No settle- ment can be permanent if it is not based upon the prin- ciples of justice to all. This Bill is unjust because it proposes to endow one particular form of religious teaching out of the rates and one only. It is true that it permits schools in towns to contract out and preserve their own religious teaching, but anyone who is . . .</p>
        <p>_ single school areas, where thousands a,re at present help- lessly priest-ridden. To those who regard "simple Bible teaching" as a sort of quasi-atheisru,it extends tolerance and liberty. I have always regarded this religious warfare in the schools as being a straggle between Protestantism and Priestcraft, end the present is a great opportunity for liberty-loving Englishmen. This Bill is by no means the last wo,d to be said on the subject, and that it will be received with unqualified hostility In some quarters, goes without saying. but I venture to think the generous spirit which has been manifested by the Government, together with this expression of national sentiment against Priestism, will go far towards providing a really national system. The outcry of dissatisfied, anti-Protestant denomina- tionalists against simple Bible teaching is, to my mind, a display of the greatest weakness for, if the ministry and parentage of the Churches exer- cise such little power over the lives of the young, that, with the combined influence of the home, the Sabbath school and the pulpit, they are unable to build the peculiar credal structure of their respective Churches upon simple Bible teaching' so much the worse for ecclesiastical organisations so far removed from the ancient, homely methods of its own religious system.To reject some such tolerant suggestion as that presented in the Bill will be to banish the Bible from our educational curriculum, and plunge the national faith into ridicule before the whole world. Councillor Fulton Smith. A careful study of the fall details of the Bill is necessary before anything but a tentative expression of opinion can be made. The intention and scope of the Bill is made unequivo- cally in the first Clause namely, "Voluntary Schools must give way to make room for Council Schools." Voluntary Schools under whatever appellation they were instituted are to be penalised for being Voluntary! In Warrington 90 per cent, of the children of school age are housed in Voluntary Schools, so in the event of , such being closed under the conditions of the New Act I accommodation for some 10,000 to 12,000 scholars will require to be provided next year:—or the children with their teachers cast adrift. The Act does not compel education authorities to employ teachers who have lost their positions through closing of Voluntary Schools, a fact of more serious import to a hardworking and till recently not over-paid pro- fession. The teachers may deem their freedom from religious tests dearly bought—when they find the market overstocked through the closing of the schools and salaries reduced in consequence. I question if the Bill as it stands commends itself to any section of the community. It is not an Education Bill but adminis- trative only, with a strong tendency towards uprooting the rights and privileges of parents, teachers and scholars, and adding enormously to the burden of the 1 over-taxed ratepayer. Education is not promoted, teachers are in a worst position and Educa- tion Authorities are invested with great responsibilities under the provisions of this measure ; and it signally fails to solve the religious problem. Rev. J. F. Matthews. The Government have assumed two things in relation to their new Education Bill. First that the people of this country want simple Bible teaching in elementary schools. Secondly that the policy of contracting out is a wise policy,and at the present time the only effective policy to be pursued. Both are disputable propositions. It follows then, that a Non possurnus was the only answer it could give to the logical and fair plea for a secular solution of a disagreeable and hateful con- troversy. The New Bill, so far as can be gathered from Mr. McKenna's speech, does not deal with the composi- tion of local authorities. The present heterogeneous bodies of that name, are only partly non-representative in character. Full popular control will never be adequately secured until either the local authority is directly elected by the ratepayers ad hoc.or its personnel is made to consist of directly elected persons. The policy of contracting out is a vicious one. The two classes of schools set up under the Bill, will make against instead of for the efficiency of education, though Mr. McKenna has given a personal assurance to the contrary. It is absurd to segregate any class of school hose the local authority. The latter becomes the only guarantee for generous treatment of schools, teachers and children. The question of religion still interferes with a truly national system of education. The parent has no right qua parent to ask for any kind of religious teaching in the common schools. if he has, then it shows that</p>
        <p>exquisite,..... ‘....., ...,,........ r ordinary business use, but it is hardly deep enough for dou ....at .e man _the,. wt ante other passages in which he revealed new beauties. His the purpose of this discussion, for the simple reason intonation was flawless and his articulation admirable.' do so. These denominational schools are to be that lots of interest is paid upon capital which remains allowed only on condition that the secular education ..o unborrowed. It is another ghastly feature of our does not suffer. Here lies the great danger. Volun- competitive system, that capital which is in use has „i tary schools have never been as efficient as Board or constantly to supply interest upon capital which lies ,4,,,-ig..,,70%.,. _l`,M4l-f -fa provided schools, and there is no probability that they idle in the Banker's hands, thus doubling the ransom I -r.r.--.:.4.',....-÷a-r- -- ever will be. If they should prove inefficient and lose paid by labour to air. Parker's "bloated capitalist." 1 A miscellaneous concert organised by Mrs. W. B. any part of the grant they will have a grievance. If My own definition of interest 1 describe as " a Roberts was given in the Parr Hall on Saturday even- they are treated leniently the children will suffer. fictitious value above par, added to capital" more in, •However, it is an experiment the Government are object f benefiting , ~,, with the praiseworthy o the . . commonly and truly called "unearned increment." willing to make in the interests of peace. If this offer funds of the Warrington Infirmary. In the pursuit of is rejected then it seems to me the. only course open to Let me now examine Mr. Parker's statement that I have given myself away. Does he conclude that this end Mrs. Roberts had the active and unstinted us is to make every school a provided school, a fair because I agree to the reward of ability, it necessarily : assistance of Captain Waring and other officers of the rent being paid for the use of the school