e imeno of p I sign at the stage wtehi:lgrehtr.eaPheerr in as this congratulated upon eleocutionary abilities of no mean controversy is the desire of the contending parties to order. In the recital of "My lady's leap" she showed look at the question not only from their own standpoint an intensity of dramatic power allied with a degree hut also from that of those who differ from them. And re restraint that would have done credit to long further that there is a strong desire to get this question practice and experience and which certainly resulted settled so that it shall no longer interfere with not only in rivetting the attention of her audience. The the progress of religion hut of education also. The grotesque drolleries of Mr. Victor Roberts caused great continuation of this strife is intolerable. amusement, evincing at the same time no little genius Councillor Stott. in the direction of a character impersonat ,r. But one „ „„li, a brief summer of y the provisions could not help thinking that the effect of Mr. Roberts's ' the Bill. seen songs, &amp;-z , would have been enhanced by being given of n le Exchequer grant gornsentreodpe redeeming feature i friaotau re ()is, thelzi a !M- -in male evening attire instead of in the nondescript tlill does not give us a nation. al system of education and and farcical costume in which he clothed himself, with fell public control. It tends to go hack to pre. Mrs. W. B. Roberts, beside conducting the ladies' 1902 conditions. It does not abolish tests for teachers. . . orchestra and leading with the violin, undertook the It perpetuates sectarianism mainly of such a character duties of piano accompanist with marked skill and as to satisfy the powerful Nonconformist element which judgment, has been driving the Government,but will not he accept- able to the Anglicans and Catholics. Although the Bill states that no child shall be compelled to attend a Voluntary school, I believe that very many will be compelled or suffer very great inconvonience. Having regard to the fact that voluntarvisra had practically broken down in Warrington at the time of the passing of the Act of 1902, I fail to see how Voluntary schools can be kept efficient without rate aid, and despite the</p>
        <p>acquainted with , the question knows that this is practi- cally an impossibility. The ordinary school of from 800 to 900 children would have to raise a sum of something between £4OO or .8300 every year, which is practically impossible, and, in addition to that, why should some people have their religious teaching free and others be called upon to pay for it ? Then again in single school areas the Bill proposes to confiscate existing schools and to give in them a kind of religious teaching which is different from that which those who built them wished f al, and in most cases,possibly,different from that which the parents who send their children to them desire. We all admit that in some single school areas the Noncon- formists have grievance. We all desire that grievance ,to be removed, but Mr. McKenna's bill seeks to remove the grievance by taking it off the shoulders of one party and putting it on to the shoulders of another. Mr. flirrell would have chastised us with whirrs. Mr. I McKenna proposes to chastise us with scorpions, but I possibly the old story may repeat itself, and the ten I tribes may refuse to be chastised at all."</p>
        <p>February 27th, 1908.</p>
        <p>Warrington Garden Suburbs.— Tbnants' Company Sir,—Replying to the letter of Jr. George Smith ' which appeared iu the Warrington Press last Saturday I I must first correct the title he gives of Garden City. I Our plan is that of Warrington Garden suburbs. It is I incorrect to speak of " a fringe of these Cities growing round our Town." The word city suggests a separate, self-contained, self-supporting community. This would be undesirable and impracticable in the case of War- rington. I have no wish to object to the word city except in so far as it may convey a wrong impression. The idea of the Tenants' Company (which should not be confused with the parent Company that has bought the land aud will act as ground landlord) is to provide houses on co-operative lines which will enable those who work in the town and are not able individually, to pro- vide homes out of the smoke, dirt and congestion which is almost inevitable in a manufacturing town. Mr. Smith asks the question What effect will this scheme have upon household property,upon shop-keepers and upon Corporation undertakings ?" In regard to the first I should anticipate that the effect on household property will be very small as the building will, com- paratively, be very gradual and will only help to provide good houses, under improved conditions, for part of the increasing population. It will incidentally tend to pre- vent advance in rents inevitable through increased land • values which always go up when the population is con- gested. It is questionable whether the erection of new house • property within the Borough is any real gain either from a sanitary, or financial point of view. While on the one hand such brings in more rates, it involves the town in proportionate additional charges, means more cost in paving, sewerage, lighting, police protection, .S‘c., and the additional children require the erection of new , schools as the present ones are practically full. Each child's school place costs in structure in new schools .213, while the annual call upon the rates for education is 12s. 6d, per child. In regard to the second point, the shop-keepers, I cannot see that it will make any difference to them. Those who at present live outside the Borough do their shopping in it, and in any further devolopment of suburbs the inhabitants will continue to buy what they want in the town. Third, Corporation undertakings. The three principal Corporation undertakings are gas, water, and tramways. The inhabitants of these suburbs will become the users of all three ; they will continue to get their water and gas from the Corporation, and will use the trams far more than they have done before. The estates already acquired, and any that may in the future be acquired, will be selected with reference to easy access to the Borough by the tramways, and it is probable that</p>
        <p>One day a microbe found a nest In a broad, expansive, full-grown chest ; He chuckled and laughed aloud with glee, " A happy home I've found," said he. "Ho ! Ho ! Mr. Microbe, not so fast, Your cosy lodging will not last, Your swift eviction I'll ensure With Woods' Great Peppermint Cure."—Advt.</p>
      </div>
