      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Creer —Randall.</p>
        <p>A marriage of considerable local interest was solemnized at the Baßiot-road Wesleyan Church, Bootle, on Wednesday, the bride being Miss Emily Isabel Randall, " Rostherne," Balliol-road,Bootle, and the bridegroom Mr. Charles Stuart Creer, 12, Morning- side-road, Bootle, Secretary of Messrs. Randall Bros Ltd., Warrington, The Rev. George Lester officiated. The bride, who wls given away by her father, Mr. Councillor George Randall, was charmingly attired in cream Brussels net, over glace silk, and a vest of embroidered Brussels net with c ,ronet of real orange blossom, myrtle leaves and white heather. She carried a lovely bouquet of white ruses, and white heather and lilies. The bridesmaids were the Misses Hilda and Barbara Randall, sisters of the bride, who wore Paris gowns of pale pink face cloth trimmed with °luny lace and pipings of , velvet, with crinoline hats to match, trimmed with roses and tulle. Their bouquets were composed of pink carnations. The bride's mother was attired in champagne face cloth, trimmed with braid to match and pale blue pipings, with hat of velvet, loses and foliage to tone with gown and sable furs. She carried a bouquet of cream roses. The bridegroom's mother was gowned in brown silk brocade, with bonnet of tulle to match, and carried a bouquet of daffodils and autumn leaves. Mr. G. Thompson discharged the duties of best man and Mr. N. Creer acted as grooms- man. Mr. E J. Marrison, of St. Andrew's, Waterloo, presided at the organ and played festal selections. After the ceremony a reception was held by the bride's parents in the hall adjoining the church, which was tastefully decorated for the occasion. During the reception selections of music were given by Miss Sara Walker's band. Later in the day the newly-married couple left for the South of England where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's going-away dress was of brown cloth with facings of black satin, and pale blue and gold braid, with brown hat to match, and fox furs.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at Tanners-lane Tannery, Warrington a presentation was made by the employees of Alessi s. Randall Bros., Ltd., to the Secretary, Mr. Creer, on the occasion of his marriage to Miss Randall,daughter of Mr. George Randall, of Bootle. After some speeches had been made, Mr. Smith, the foreman, on behalf of the workmen, asked Mr. John Randall, managing director, to present Mr. Creer with a beautiful clock and ornaments to match, as a token of their esteem and regard. Mr. Creer c.rdially responded.</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Ouugas sad Colds ; never fails. 1/li, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PHIL SQUOD.</head>
        <p>PHIL SQUOD.</p>
        <p>CONCERT IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>OLD-AGE PENSIONS BILL INTRODUCED.</p>
        <p>Home Rule the Next Measure.</p>
        <p>GOVERNMENT PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Mr. AWBERY said be was desired by the Premier to state that it was proposed to resume the discussion on the Old Age Pensions Bill next Thursday. The following week the hall was occupied and they would again have to meet on Wednesday. The Government had intended on that night (March 11th) to introduce the Education Bill but it would be impossible for the Minister of Education to be present, so the measure would have to be placed lower in the list. In place of that Bill, it was his (Mr. Awbery's) intention—and he was sorry to make the statement to empty Nationalist benches—to introduce a measure of Home Rule for Ireland on March llth and the debate would be extended to March 19th. On March 26th the Education Bill would be introduced and two nights would be devoted to it. That would bring them to April 2nd and he hoped before then there would he a consultation as to the advisability of extending the session beyond that date.</p>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSIONS. Mr. J. MORTON, Battersea, (President of the Local Government Board) then introduced the Old Age Pensions Bill, which provides that on and after June Ist, 1908, every British subject who has resided within the United Kingdom for 40 years and who has attained the age of 65 years shall be entitled to receive by a fund created and maintained by MO/14,S provided by Parliament and called the State Pension 141und, a sum of ss. per week for life, the pension to be payable through the Post Office. He said the Bill was drawn on such a moderate scale that he hoped it would commend itself to the House. He proceeded to recall the history of the agitation for pensions, and said it was an unsatis- factory and deplorable fact that so large a proportion of the working classes were in old age in receipt of poor law relief. The problem was a vital and pzessing one. As the agitation had gone ahead the theory based on the contributory idea seemed to have receded into the background, and the principle which Sir Charles Booth had adopted was the predominant view of the day. The principle adopted by the Government in the Bill before the House was that of a non-contributory and non-discriminatory scheme. Accordinr, to the census of 1901, if pensions were granted to all over 65, it would entail the Government providing for something 2,018,000 pensioners—that was without making any exceptions—at a cost of £26,246,000 annually. The question of raising the money appeared to be the great obstacle in many people's minds. He would, however, point out that between 1853 and 1905 the extra expenditure on the army and navy amounted to no less than £30,600,000. The old-age pensions grant would not be a now burden because we had already the poor with Us and they had to be kept. It simply meant that the Bill would alter the channels by which the 26 millions was raised, concentrate it in the hands of the Government, and see that the poor had a little to tide over their declining years. The difficulty in regard to raising the money would he felt chiefly alien the tax in whatever form was actually put into operation for the first time, but when things had settled down to their normal condition there would be little differenee. There had been an idea that by making it rather awkward for the well-to-do to get their pensions they might induce them to forego them, but he could scarcely conceive of that being an honest deal. Under the Government Bill the well-to-do could share, as they ought to do, in the allocation of the fund. He denied that pensions would discourage thrift for if the workers had the certainty of a modicum of comfort there would be all the more incentive for them to make additions to it. i Mr. P. PEACOCK (Wirral), seconded the adoption of the Bill and said something most be done, and at once. Two-fifths of the adult population of Great Britain consisted of agricultural labourers, unskilled workmen and women workers. From these came SO per cent, of our paupers and two out of every three who survived to the age of 65 went to the Poor Law for relief. The Poor Law system, he contended, was a cruel treatment and a scientific failure. In adopting a non-dis- criminatory scheme the Government would rather err on the side of those who did not deserve it than allow those who should participate in the benefits to go with- out. They must remove the reproach that in the United Kingdom the aged poor were worse treated than in any other European country. By giving pensions they would lengthen the lives of the aged and the financial burden would come heavily upon them, but if they were short of money the Liberal party was prepared to tax a little bit more heavily the 5,000,000 people out of 43,000,000, who took one half the income of the country and did little for it. (Ministerial cheers.) Counoillor NIELD caused some amusement by remark- ing that the addresses of the previous speakers did more credit to their hearts thou to their legislative capacity. He had been puzzled as to whether the House was entitled to call the measure a Bill or whether it should not be regarded as a pious resolution. With regard to the principle of old-age pensions the Conservatives were absolutely committed to it, and they would not allow the Government side of the House to possess a monopoly of hearts. (" Oh " and cheers.) They were determined to do all that was possible for the aged poor, but it was a question of ways and means. The Government had absolutely ignored the question of where the revenue was to come from. " A friend of mine suggested " continued the speaker " but I think he was fresh from the pantomime, that the Government : had found Aladdin's Cave, or that the Secretary for the Colonies in his ramblings through Africa must have come across Solomon's mine. ' (Laughter.) When I Councillor Nield announced his intention of ",coming 1 The Soldiers' Memorial. seriously to the Bill " there were loud cries of ' Bear, I Yesterday was Ladystnith Day and honour was done hear." He contended that the Bill was most loosely ! t Ithe memory of the soldiers who fell in the Sluth conceived—in fact it was prematurely born. If it had I r A African War by the placing of some beautiful flora/ been two months later, when the Chancellor of the I Exchequer had produced his Budget, they would then : tributes round the base of the memorial in gieen' have been shown how the Government proposed to find i Gardens. The foot of the statue was decorated witla the milk with which to feed the child. (Laughter.) , a border of flowers sent by the officers of the Soutbt Hs moved that the Bill be read that day six months. I Lancashire Regiment, and at the base of the pedestal. Mr. Smut" (North Bedfordshire) said nobody wae ' were wreaths from : —Colonel. J. A. Edelsten and : more in favour of old-age pensions that he was. Wd ! officers of the Ist V.B. (P.W.V ) South Lancashire, I pensioned our Civil Service and Local Government Boars , Regiment i officers of the depot of the South Lanes officials, Cabinet Ministers, soldiers, sailors—in fact ! n ; ) everybody but the man who paid for those pensions. shire Regiment officers of the 2nd S uth Lancashire (Hear, hear.) He would not, however' support the Bill Regiment ; staff-sergants and sergeants of the Ist las it was so vague and unconditional. He argued in V.B. Smith Lancashire Regiment; the sergeants of tiler I favour of a discriminatory Bill which would also enforce depot South Lancashire Regiment; sergeants of ther a contribution from those who would receive the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment ; Captain F. Di- pensions. Under the Government measure criminals , who had been maintained by the State for many years , Appleton. , i would be entitled to pensions. (Hear, hear.) He , i suggested, too, that they ought to exclude fraudulent ; I bankrupts from the advantages of the measure. There 1 - I was nothing either to prevent a man already receiving a Government pension from claiming the additional ss. a ' week. He advocated that the Goye-nment should CRT:ER—RAND A.LL—On February 256, at Balliol-' supplement the amounts saved by the people for pension Wesleyan Church, Bootle, by the Rev. George Le&amp; with an equal amount, the maximum to be .£1 a week. 1 Charles Stuart Creer, 12. Morningside-road, 800 l 1 Mr. FAULKNER (Hyde) who repeatedly addressed the (secretary of Randall Bros. Ltd., Warrington) I House as "dear friends " said old age pensions had been Emily Isabel, daughter of Mr. Councillor Geo i too long delayed and too long promised by the Tory Randall," Rostherne," Balliol-road, Bootle. ! party. In 1895 the Tories got into power on a promise ito provide old-age pensions—(cheers and "No )—but . they had only played with the question. It would, he Warrington.—Printed and published by ,i 0142,7 held, be impossible and useless to discriminate, because es Co., at The Examiner General Printing Worn the very ch.sracters they would exclude from the Bewsey-street,