      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST (King's iVinsford) addressed his speech to " Mr. Speaker and friends " when The SPEAKER interposed with a hint that members in referring to the other side would call them " hon members.' Councillor BROADHURST : r wish to draw your attention, my honourable friends —( laughter) —to the fact that the Government are entirely failing to cater for one class of people—the poor people who become aged at forty-five. These honoure do members— (laughter) —I say honourable friends. (More laughter.) The SPEAKER appealed to the House to allow the hon. member to proceed. Councillor BROADHURST : 1 hope you will bear with rue a little, friends, for it is a most important address lam going to give you. (Laughter.) Continuing, the speaker called forth Socialist Hear, hears "by con- tending that the present competitive system resulted its lack of employment at a comparatively early age. As to the ways and means it was left to Tariff reformers. and Protectionists to teach the Government the way. Councillor Broadhurst then gave a long statement as to what had been done in Germany, and a quotation from a speech by the Kaiser was greeted with cries of " Good old William." Mr. E. ENor.axn (Warrington, Chancellor of the Exchequer) said that judging by the remarkable exodus of strapping young people from Germany, it was not a. particularly desirable country to live in. The Leader of the Opposition had twitted the Government for not showing how the 21 millions were to be raised, but the Government were prepared to easily raise a much larger sum. (Ministerial Cheers.) The late Tory Government in spite of their pious resolutions of which the leader of the Opposition spoke so glibly, were the great care- takers of the rich classes and when in power forgot the workers. '(Cheers.) They had shirked the question of old-age pensions and it seemed to him they did not want to find ways and means. A mention of Mr. Chamber- lain's name was greeted with cheers from the Opposition and Mr. England retorted " Yes, a great mind that went wrong some years ago. (Laughter.) We all sympathise with him in his illness and wish him a speedy recovery. (Hear. hear.) But still I say he is a lost mind. Although , he stated time after time on the platform that he, was in favour of old-age pensions when he had the opportunity he never seemed to embrace it." This great Government, continued Mr. England amidst cheers and dissent, were not only tackling the problem but were going to accomplish the work in spite of the Opposition. (Cheers.) As to ways and means, he proposed to raise by the death duties £2i,000,000, for at present the duties on £300,000,000 left in sue year produced only £14,000,000. The excise duties only brought in £30,000,000 and he should allocate a few millions from that source. He would try to elevate the spirits of the people by taxing their spirits. (Laughter and applause.) Councillor BaLr. (West Wolverhampton' assured the Government that the Socialists were prepared to give them every assistance in passing the Bill. (Cheers.) He desired, however, to oiler friendly crfticisms in the hope that next week amendments would be accepted to make the Bill more valuable. The Chancellor of the Exchequer had indicated the lines on which it was quite easy to obtain the money and some of his friends would move an amendment next week with the object of specially allocating to the pension fend now revenue obtained by death duties, income tax, &amp;c. fie assumed the Bill did not require a man to live 40 consecutive, years in the country in order to gain a pension, as that. would be unjust to many who had to work abroad forc a time. Sneaking of the universal application of the. measure, Councillor Ball said : " I have a vision of the Duke of Westminster walking up to the Post Moe and drawing his ss. a week. (Laughter.) I imagine he would draw it quarterly and that he would not ask for it in advance." (Laughter.) The way in which the money could be raised, he continued, was so obvious that he was surprised the members of the Opposition boggled so much at it. Councillor Ball caused some amusement by stating that old-age pensions ought tns appeal particularly to the Tories because so far back as 1553, in the reign of Edward VI. a pension scheme was formed, and they should have made up their minds now that something definite should be done. Mr. G. H. W. STEEL (Northwich) said the Conserve-. tive party's solution of the problem of way and means was tariff reform. (Laughter.) That seemed to cause, a lot of humour but the day was coming when the country would declare in no uncertain voice for tariff reform. (" No, No.") The Government had not intro- duced a workable measure. Mr. Steel twitted the Government on being too Puritanical to tax book- makers whilst not hesitating to accept revenue from the drink traffic. " Their Puritanical notions," he exclaimed. "are all for show. Don't run away with the idea that all the goodness is on that side of the House." (Laughter Mr. NV. exantEs (S. Birmingham) said that not a, single leader of the Conservative party had ever said rt. word against old-age pensions. He knew uf no previous instance of a Government bringing in a Bill involving very largo expenditure without telling the country hovg they were going to find the money. At this stage Mr. W. L. WILLIAMS (oswestry) moved the adjournment of the debate and the House went into commitee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>• – ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>• - ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Dick Whittington."</p>
        <p>Pantomime once more holds sway at this theatre. Mr. James Kiddie's Company appealing in the ever— popular "Dick Whittington. There 'lava been large, audiences nightly and they have joined with great gusto in the choruses of the many faymirite songs which are given, the performance going with a good swing throughout. Miss Lena Pages is a vivacious principal boy and scores a success in her songs, one of the best being " teroplane for two." Miss Nellie Merton, the principal. girl, speedily wins the favour of the audience, her singing and dancing being admirable. Miss Florence, Hilton, who is described as "the pocket edition of Vesta Tilley " gives an excellent impersonation of Sir ; Algernon de Nlash,a wealthy gallant,which is the great "hit" of the pantomitne,and she has to respond to many encores. The humorous element is, of course, well to. the fore, Mr. Spencer being especially laughable as the, cook. Jr. Harry Plean, a well-known comedian, figures as Idle Jack and contributes much to the, general merriment, and the Hagnaway Bros are, responsible for fun of the knockabout type. The* scenery is on an elaborate scale and some very pleasing effects are obtained. There are pretty dances. the La Belle Troupe excelling in this respect, and. altogether the pantomime is highly entertaining. There will be a matinee this (Saturday) afternooa at 2.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>A. treat is promised the most critical of theatre- goers for next week, when the well-known actor Mr. Norman V. Norman, will pay a first visit to the Royal Court Theatre, with his principal company including Miss Beatrice Wilson, the popular actress. Three exceedingly attractive plays are promised during the. visit —" The Adventure of Lady Ursula "(from the Duke of York's Theatre, London, by special arrange- ment with Mr. Charles Frohman) ; " Mr. Sheridan,'" now MI its first provincial tour, direct from the Garrick Theatre, London, by arrangsment with Mr. Arthur Bourchier,) and " David Garrick." Three performances of " Lady Ursula " will be given on Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday with. Miss Beatrice Wilson as " Lads- Ursida Barrini- ton " and Mr. Norman as " Sir George Sylvester. "David Garrick" will be played twice, on Thursday and Satprday, with Mr. Norman in the title-role, andt Miss \\Tison as the Alderman's daughter. "Garrick' evenings offer another attraction in new one-act play entitled " A Privy Council " which is also (like 'Mr. Sheridan") on its first provincial tour. " Mr. Sheridan'" on Friday will complete a very entertaining week's bill.</p>
      </div>
